---
// THE REPLAY INSTRUMENT. Added 2026-09-17, rebuilt the same day.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS. Four of the five sector pages already promised an evaluator a
// worked alarm output and a worked modification record. Both existed only as
// sentences. This is those two, shown, from an archived run.
//
// WHY IT WAS REBUILT. The first version drew two accuracy charts side by side:
// a page whose thesis is "the black box is opened" rendering two boxes with a
// number written on the outside of each. A reader who had never seen a neural
// network learned only that one line ended higher than the other. The network
// itself — the thing the whole argument is about — was never drawn.
//
// WHAT IT DRAWS NOW. The network. Three convolutional stages at their real
// shapes, the signal passing through them, each stage's health as a level you
// can watch fall and recover, and the bounded four-operation chain descending
// into the shared bottleneck. The accuracy curve is one strip underneath, with
// both arms on a single axis — evidence that the repair worked, not the exhibit.
//
// BOTH PANES DRAW THE SAME ANATOMY. Same stages, same heights, same positions,
// because it is the same network. The left one is sealed; the right one is cut
// open. That is the honest form of the claim: nothing was withheld from the
// baseline, the quantity was never computed.
//
// IT RUNS ON A DARK GROUND, alone on a light site. Telemetry reads as emitted
// signal against dark, which is why every instrument console is dark, and it
// separates the exhibit from the page's prose. This is equipment, not a figure.
//
// NOTHING COMPUTES IN THE BROWSER. Every value is read from a bundle generated
// from the experiment archive by scripts/build-replay-bundle.py and asserted
// against every published figure by scripts/verify-replay-bundle.py. Stage fill
// is `goodness`; an emptied stage is `weight_norm == 0`, which is literally what
// the archive records at the lesion; strikes are real correction events at their
// real steps. There is no decorative data on this page.
export interface Props {
	scenario?: string;
	/** Hide the scenario selector when embedded on a page about one feature. */
	fixed?: boolean;
	/** Open on the record — used on /product/diary. */
	openRecord?: boolean;
	/** Restart when the run finishes, instead of ending on the record.
	 *  True where the replay is ambient — an embed inside a page about
	 *  something else. False on /demo, where the run IS the subject and the
	 *  record is the payoff a reader came for. */
	loop?: boolean;
	compact?: boolean;
	/** 'auto' opens the guided read by itself, once per browser, when the component first
	 *  scrolls into view. For the page whose whole subject is this component. Everywhere
	 *  else the replay is an illustration inside a page about something else, and a tour
	 *  that starts itself there is an interruption. */
	guide?: 'auto' | 'manual';
	/** Render as a full-height application screen — a rail, a metric strip and a working
	 *  area — instead of a block inside a page. For /black-box only, where the component IS
	 *  the page rather than an illustration inside one. */
	app?: boolean;
	/** App mode only: the breadcrumb, rendered into the product bar. It lives here rather
	 *  than in a PageBanner because in app mode there is no banner — and dropping it would
	 *  cost the page its BreadcrumbList, which is the one rich result this site is eligible
	 *  for. Same markup and aria-label as the banner's, so the schema guard still sees it. */
	trail?: Array<{ label: string; href?: string }>;
}
const {
	scenario = 'dead-layer-lesion',
	fixed = false,
	openRecord = false,
	loop = false,
	compact = false,
	guide = 'manual',
	app = false,
	trail = [],
} = Astro.props;

const SCENARIOS = [
	{ key: 'dead-layer-lesion', label: 'Dead-layer lesion' },
	{ key: 'full-hierarchy', label: 'Full-hierarchy lesion' },
	{ key: 'label-noise', label: 'Corrupted labels' },
	{ key: 'adversarial', label: 'Adversarial weight injection' },

];
---

<section
	class={`rp${compact ? ' rp--compact' : ''}${app ? ' rp--app' : ''}`}
	data-replay
	data-scenario={scenario}
	data-open-record={openRecord ? 'true' : 'false'}
	data-loop={loop ? 'true' : 'false'}
	data-guide-mode={guide}
>
	{/* ── APPLICATION CHROME ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
	    Only in app mode. A product bar, a rail and a metric strip: the three things that
	    make a screen read as an instrument someone works in rather than a figure embedded
	    in an article.

	    THE RAIL IS THE SCENARIO PICKER AND THE PANEL SWITCHER, which is why the <select>
	    and the tablist are hidden in this mode rather than duplicated — two controls driving
	    one piece of state is how they drift apart. The rail writes to the same handlers.

	    The rail collapses to icons before the working area gets too narrow for the two
	    networks: anatomy.ts holds each to 560px, so the pair needs 1120px and the rail has
	    to give its 220px back long before that limit is reached. */}
	<div class="rp-console">
	{/* THE ORIENTATION STRIPE. The first band inside the instrument is a sentence, not
	    chrome. Before this, a first-time reader met the product bar — which carries
	    "DEAD-LAYER LESION" and "ablation_ormas_cnn_dead_s0" — and then four figures with
	    no units a stranger knows, and only reached plain language in the fourth band down.
	    The explanation now comes before the jargon it explains.

	    This REPLACES .rp-plain in app mode rather than joining it: two prose bands on one
	    screen is how a reader learns to skip both, and the vertical budget here is already
	    tight. .rp-plain still renders everywhere else the component is used. */}
	{app && (
		<div class="rp-orient">
			<p class="rp-orient-line">
				{/* The {' '} are load-bearing. Astro drops the whitespace at an element→text line
				    boundary, so `…sealed network</b>` followed by `on the left` on the next line
				    rendered as "sealed networkon the left". A margin on the element papers over
				    the gap visually but leaves the word joined for a screen reader and for
				    select-and-copy, so the space is a real space. */}
				<strong>Two networks, the same data, the same damage.</strong>{' '}
				<b class="rp-orient-open">ORMAS</b>, on the right, names which parts broke — two
				steps later — and repairs itself. The <b class="rp-orient-sealed">sealed network</b>{' '}
				on the left cannot say anything about it, and never will. Every figure is read
				from one archived training run.
			</p>
		</div>
	)}

	{app && (
		<header class="rp-appbar">
			<p class="rp-appbar-mark">ORMAS<span>·</span>replay console</p>
			{trail.length > 0 && (
				<nav class="rp-appbar-trail" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
					<ol>
						{trail.map((c, i) => (
							<li>
								{c.href && i < trail.length - 1
									? <a href={c.href}>{c.label}</a>
									: <span aria-current="page">{c.label}</span>}
							</li>
						))}
					</ol>
				</nav>
			)}
			<p class="rp-opts-label" id="rp-opts-label">Scenario</p>
			<div class="rp-opts" role="group" aria-labelledby="rp-opts-label">
				{SCENARIOS.map((s) => (
					<button
						type="button"
						class="rp-opt"
						data-rail-scenario={s.key}
						aria-pressed={s.key === scenario}
					>{s.label}</button>
				))}
			</div>
			<p class="rp-appbar-run" data-conditions-app>Loading the archived run…</p>
		</header>
	)}

	<div class="rp-shell">
		{/* NO RAIL. It cost 220px of the working area to hold eight items that fit on one line,
		    and it made the panels one-at-a-time when the whole point of this screen is that
		    the two networks and their four readouts are compared AT ONCE. The scenarios moved
		    into the product bar as options; the panels are all on screen together. */}
		<div class="rp-main">
			{app && (
				<div class="rp-metrics" data-metrics>
					<div class="rp-metric">
						<p class="rp-metric-label">Sealed network</p>
						<p class="rp-metric-value" data-metric-sealed>—</p>
						<p class="rp-metric-note">test accuracy now</p>
					</div>
					<div class="rp-metric rp-metric--open">
						<p class="rp-metric-label">Open network</p>
						<p class="rp-metric-value" data-metric-open>—</p>
						<p class="rp-metric-note">test accuracy now</p>
					</div>
					<div class="rp-metric">
						<p class="rp-metric-label">Corrections written</p>
						<p class="rp-metric-value" data-metric-corrections>—</p>
						<p class="rp-metric-note">during training, so far</p>
					</div>
					<div class="rp-metric">
						<p class="rp-metric-label">Fault named in</p>
						<p class="rp-metric-value" data-metric-lag>—</p>
						<p class="rp-metric-note" data-metric-lag-note>after the event</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			)}

	<header class="rp-bar">
		<div class="rp-bar-left">
			<p class="rp-legend-item">The same network, twice. One sealed, one open.</p>
			{!fixed ? (
				<label class="rp-pick">
					<span class="rp-pick-label">Scenario</span>
					<select class="rp-select" data-select>
						{SCENARIOS.map((s) => (
							<option value={s.key} selected={s.key === scenario}>{s.label}</option>
						))}
					</select>
				</label>
			) : (
				<p class="rp-title" data-title>—</p>
			)}
		</div>
		<p class="rp-run" data-conditions>Loading the archived run…</p>
	</header>

	{/* PLAIN LANGUAGE, FIRST. Everything else in this component is written for somebody who
	    already knows what an epoch is. A first-time reader who does not gets a wall of
	    instrumentation and no reason to press anything, which is what was reported. Two
	    sentences, no jargon, above the diagram rather than below it. */}
	{/* Shown only when the run cannot be fetched. Static markup with no display rule of its
	    own, so reset.css's [hidden] governs it. */}
	<div class="rp-failed" data-failed hidden role="alert">
		<p class="rp-failed-title">The archived run did not load.</p>
		<p class="rp-failed-body">
			Every figure on this screen is read from a file in the experiment archive, and that
			file did not arrive. Nothing here is computed in the browser, so there is nothing to
			show until it does.
		</p>
		<button type="button" class="rp-btn rp-btn--primary" data-retry>Try again</button>
	</div>

	{/* Non-app only — in app mode the orientation stripe above the product bar carries this. */}
	{!app && (
		<p class="rp-plain">
			<strong>A neural network is damaged partway through training.</strong> The version on the
			right names which parts broke, two steps later, and repairs itself. The one on the left
			cannot say anything about it, and never will.
		</p>
	)}

	<div class="rp-stageband">
		<div class="rp-panes">
			<article class="rp-pane rp-pane--sealed">
				<div class="rp-anatomy" data-anatomy-baseline></div>
			</article>
			<article class="rp-pane rp-pane--open">
				<div class="rp-anatomy" data-anatomy-ormas></div>
			</article>
		</div>
	</div>

	<p class="rp-beat" data-beat hidden></p>

	<div class="rp-controls">
		<button type="button" class="rp-btn rp-btn--play" data-play aria-label="Play">
			<span data-play-label>Play</span>
		</button>
		<label class="rp-scrub">
			<span class="rp-sr">Epoch</span>
			<input type="range" data-scrub min="0" max="199" value="0" step="1" />
		</label>
		<output class="rp-epoch" data-epoch>epoch 0 / 199</output>
		<div class="rp-rates" role="group" aria-label="Playback speed">
			{[1, 4, 16].map((r) => (
				<button type="button" class="rp-rate" data-rate={r} aria-pressed={r === 1}>{r}×</button>
			))}
		</div>
		<button type="button" class="rp-btn rp-btn--event" data-replay-event>Replay from the event</button>
		{/* THE GUIDED READ. Opt-in, never automatic: the ambient homepage embed hides this
		    button entirely (see the loop branch in the island), because a tour that starts
		    itself over a looping background animation is an interruption, not guidance. */}
		<button type="button" class="rp-btn rp-btn--guide" data-guide-start>Guided read</button>
		<p class="rp-nature" data-nature></p>
	</div>

	{/* The annotation card is STATIC MARKUP, not injected at runtime. Astro scopes styles by
	    appending a [data-astro-cid-…] attribute to every selector, and an element created in
	    JavaScript never carries it — twice now that has shipped as a silently unstyled panel.
	    Only text content and hidden/class state are touched from the island. */}
	<aside class="rp-guide" data-guide hidden aria-live="polite">
		{/* The rail runs the full width across the top: every stop visible at once, the
		    current one filled, each reachable. Dots told a reader their position and nothing
		    else — not how far it runs, and not how to get back to one they had passed. */}
		<nav class="rp-guide-rail" data-guide-rail aria-label="Guided read progress"></nav>
		<div class="rp-guide-text">
			<p class="rp-guide-eyebrow">
				<span data-guide-count></span><span data-guide-where></span>
				<span class="rp-guide-live" data-guide-live></span>
			</p>
			<h4 class="rp-guide-title" data-guide-title></h4>
			<p class="rp-guide-body" data-guide-body></p>
		</div>
		<div class="rp-guide-foot">
			<button type="button" class="rp-guide-skip" data-guide-skip>Skip<span class="rp-guide-esc">esc</span></button>
			<button type="button" class="rp-btn" data-guide-back>Back</button>
			<button type="button" class="rp-btn rp-btn--primary" data-guide-next>Next</button>
		</div>
	</aside>

	{/* ── ONE PANEL AT A TIME ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
	    The chart, the ledger, the answer table and the record used to stack, and the
	    component came to 1113px against 811px of usable height at 1440×900. Everything a
	    reader was asked to compare was therefore off-screen from everything else, and every
	    problem reported on this component — the guided read with nowhere to sit, the dock
	    hunting for room, having to scroll to see what changed — was downstream of that one
	    number.

	    Tabbed, the component is bar 63 + panes 336 + controls 71 + panel 291 = 761px. It
	    fits, with room to spare.

	    UNDER the panes, not beside them. The side-by-side arrangement is the obvious one and
	    it cannot work here: anatomy.ts holds each network to MIN_W = 560, so the two need
	    1120px between them, and a right-hand panel taking a third of a 1280px container
	    would leave them 395px each and force an overflow.

	    "What each one can answer" is the default tab deliberately. It is the only panel a
	    non-technical reader understands on sight — a list of questions, and whether each
	    network can answer them. The chart and the ledger are for someone already convinced. */}
	<div class="rp-tabs">
		<div class="rp-tablist" role="tablist" aria-label="Replay panels">
			<button type="button" class="rp-tab" role="tab" id="rp-tab-answers" aria-controls="rp-panel-answers" aria-selected="true" data-tab="answers">What each one can answer</button>
			<button type="button" class="rp-tab" role="tab" id="rp-tab-accuracy" aria-controls="rp-panel-accuracy" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1" data-tab="accuracy">Accuracy</button>
			<button type="button" class="rp-tab" role="tab" id="rp-tab-ledger" aria-controls="rp-panel-ledger" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1" data-tab="ledger">Corrections</button>
			<button type="button" class="rp-tab" role="tab" id="rp-tab-record" aria-controls="rp-panel-record" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1" data-tab="record">The record</button>
		</div>

		<div class="rp-panel" id="rp-panel-answers" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="rp-tab-answers" data-panel="answers">
			<div class="rp-verdict">
			<p class="rp-chart-label">What each one can answer, at this epoch</p>
			<table class="rp-verdict-table">
				<thead>
					<tr>
						<th scope="col">The question</th>
						<th scope="col">Standard CNN</th>
						<th scope="col">ORMAS</th>
					</tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody data-verdict></tbody>
			</table>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="rp-panel" id="rp-panel-accuracy" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="rp-tab-accuracy" data-panel="accuracy" hidden>
			<div class="rp-chart">
			<p class="rp-chart-label">Test accuracy · both arms, one axis</p>
			<div class="rp-chart-box">
				<canvas data-canvas aria-label="Test accuracy across training for both networks on one axis"></canvas>
			</div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="rp-panel" id="rp-panel-ledger" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="rp-tab-ledger" data-panel="ledger" hidden>
			<div class="rp-ledger">
			<p class="rp-chart-label">Correction ledger · written during training</p>
			<div class="rp-alarms" data-alarms></div>
			</div>
		</div>
		</div>
		</div>
	</div>
	</div>{/* /.rp-console — the one-screen console: stripe + product bar + working area */}

	{/* THE RECORD IS THE ENDING, not a quarter of the readout row. In a 423px cell its
	    five-column table had nowhere to go and the panel sat mostly empty; as a full-width
	    strip under the console the columns breathe and it reads as what it is — the artefact
	    the whole screen exists to produce. It is below the fold on purpose: the console is
	    one screen, and this is the first thing under it. */}
	<div class="rp-panel rp-recordband" id="rp-panel-record" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="rp-tab-record" data-panel="record" hidden>
	<div class="rp-record" data-record>
	<header class="rp-record-head">
		<h4 class="rp-record-title">The record</h4>
		<p class="rp-record-sub" data-record-sub></p>
	</header>
	<dl class="rp-record-grid" data-record-grid></dl>
	<div class="rp-table-wrap">
		<table class="rp-table">
			<caption class="rp-sr">Every correction, with its component, diagnosis, step, magnitude and declared ceiling</caption>
			<thead>
				<tr><th scope="col">Step</th><th scope="col">Component</th><th scope="col">Diagnosis</th><th scope="col">Magnitude</th><th scope="col">Ceiling</th></tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody data-record-rows></tbody>
		</table>
	</div>
	<div class="rp-record-actions">
		<button type="button" class="rp-btn rp-btn--primary" data-download>Download the record</button>
		<button type="button" class="rp-btn" data-diff>Diff against the pre-event version</button>
	</div>
	<div class="rp-diff" data-diff-panel hidden></div>
	</div>
	</div>

	<noscript>
		<p class="rp-noscript">
			This replay needs JavaScript. The underlying figures are on the technology
			page, with the conditions each was measured under.
		</p>
	</noscript>
</section>

<script>
	import { ReplayEngine } from '../scripts/replay/engine';
	import { draw, readTheme, sizeCanvas } from '../scripts/replay/render';
	import { Anatomy } from '../scripts/replay/anatomy';
	import type { Bundle, Correction } from '../scripts/replay/types';
	import { buildGuide, type GuideStep } from '../scripts/replay/guide';

	const DIAGNOSIS: Record<string, string> = {
		dead: 'dead', oscillating: 'oscillating', saturated: 'saturated',
		loss_stagnant: 'loss stagnant', gradient_dead: 'gradient dead',
		low_confidence: 'low confidence', exploded: 'exploded',
	};

	const reduced = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;
	const pct = (v: number) => `${(v * 100).toFixed(1)}%`;
	const num = (v: number) => v.toLocaleString('en-GB');
	const teardown: Array<() => void> = [];

	function init(root: HTMLElement) {
		if (root.dataset.replayReady === 'true') return;
		root.dataset.replayReady = 'true';

		const q = <T extends HTMLElement>(s: string) => root.querySelector(s) as T | null;
		const canvas = q<HTMLCanvasElement>('[data-canvas]')!;
		const elRun = q('[data-conditions]')!;
		const elTitle = q('[data-title]');
		const elAlarms = q('[data-alarms]')!;
		const elBeat = q('[data-beat]')!;
		const elEpoch = q('[data-epoch]')!;
		const elScrub = q<HTMLInputElement>('[data-scrub]')!;
		const btnPlay = q<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-play]')!;
		const lblPlay = q('[data-play-label]')!;
		const btnEvent = q<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-replay-event]')!;
		const select = q<HTMLSelectElement>('[data-select]');
		const elRecord = q('[data-record]')!;
		const elGuide = q('[data-guide]')!;
		const btnGuide = q<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-guide-start]')!;

		const anaBase = new Anatomy(q('[data-anatomy-baseline]')!, { instrumented: false, reduced });
		const anaOrmas = new Anatomy(q('[data-anatomy-ormas]')!, { instrumented: true, reduced });

		let engine: ReplayEngine | null = null;
		let bundle: Bundle | null = null;
		let theme = readTheme(root);
		const recordAlwaysOn = root.classList.contains('rp--app');
		let recordShown = -1;

		function render(epoch: number) {
			if (!bundle || !engine) return;
			const e = Math.round(epoch);
			paintMetrics(epoch);

			// THE RECORD FILLS AS THE RUN GOES. It used to be written once, on the run
			// ending, because it was a panel you opened afterwards. As a strip that is
			// always on screen, an empty table under a live console reads as broken.
			// Re-rendered only when the count changes — it is 85 rows, and rebuilding them
			// on every frame would be the most expensive thing on the page.
			if (recordAlwaysOn && bundle) {
				const n = bundle.corrections.filter((c) => (c.epoch ?? 0) <= e).length;
				if (n !== recordShown) { recordShown = n; renderRecord(e); }
			}

			draw(canvas, bundle, epoch, theme);

			const frame = bundle.health.find((f) => f.epoch === e)
				?? bundle.health.filter((f) => f.epoch <= e).pop()
				?? bundle.health[0] ?? null;

			// A correction pulses on the stage it names, for the epoch it fired in.
			const striking = new Set<number>();
			for (const c of bundle.corrections) {
				if (c.epoch === e) striking.add(c.node);
			}
			anaOrmas.update(frame, striking, engine.isPlaying);
			anaBase.update(null, new Set(), engine.isPlaying);

			elEpoch.textContent = `epoch ${e} / ${engine.lastEpoch}`;
			if (document.activeElement !== elScrub) elScrub.value = String(e);
			renderAlarms(e);
			renderBeat(e);
			renderVerdict(e, frame);
		}

		/* The comparison, stated rather than implied. Every row is answered from
		   the frame on screen. The last row is the important one: the aggregate
		   IS available to both, and saying so is what keeps the other four
		   rows credible — the baseline is not blind to everything, it is blind
		   to everything beneath the aggregate. */
		function renderVerdict(epoch: number, frame: ReturnType<typeof Object> | null) {
			if (!bundle || !engine) return;
			const ev = bundle.event;
			const fired = ev != null && epoch >= ev.epoch;
			const f = frame as { nodes: Array<{ id: number; weight_norm: number | null }> } | null;

			const down = f ? f.nodes.filter((n) => (n.weight_norm ?? 0) === 0).map((n) => n.id) : [];
			const seen = bundle.corrections.filter((c) => c.epoch !== null && c.epoch <= epoch);
			const last = seen[seen.length - 1];

			const oAcc = bundle.series.ormas.accuracy[Math.min(epoch, engine.lastEpoch)];
            const bAcc = bundle.series.baseline.accuracy[Math.min(epoch, engine.lastEpoch)];

			const none = '<span class="rp-v-none">no such quantity exists</span>';
			const rows: Array<[string, string, string]> = [
				[
					'Which component is failing?',
					none,
					down.length
						? `<span class="rp-v-hit">${down.map((i) => `stage ${i}`).join(', ')} — dead</span>`
						: (last ? `stage ${last.node} — ${DIAGNOSIS[last.diagnosis] ?? last.diagnosis}` : 'none flagged'),
				],
				[
					'At which step?',
					ev ? (fired ? '<span class="rp-v-none">not until the loss moves</span>' : none)
					   : '<span class="rp-v-none">not until the curve turns down</span>',
					last ? `<span class="rp-v-hit">${num(last.step)}</span>` : '—',
				],
				[
					'By how much?',
					none,
					last?.magnitude != null
						? `magnitude ${last.magnitude.toFixed(4)}${last.ceiling != null ? `, ceiling ${last.ceiling.toFixed(2)} w` : ''}`
						: '—',
				],
				[
					ev ? 'What was done about it?' : 'What was done about the bad labels?',
					'<span class="rp-v-none">nothing — the run continues</span>',
					seen.length
						? `<span class="rp-v-hit">${seen.length} bounded correction${seen.length === 1 ? '' : 's'}</span>, each inside its declared ceiling`
						: 'nothing yet',
				],
				[
					'Test accuracy right now',
					`<span class="rp-v-both">${pct(bAcc)}</span>`,
					`<span class="rp-v-both">${pct(oAcc)}</span>`,
				],
			];

			const tb = q('[data-verdict]');
			if (tb) {
				tb.innerHTML = rows.map(([qn, a, b]) =>
					`<tr><th scope="row">${qn}</th><td>${a}</td><td>${b}</td></tr>`).join('');
			}
		}

		function renderAlarms(epoch: number) {
			if (!bundle) return;
			const seen = bundle.corrections.filter((c) => c.epoch !== null && c.epoch <= epoch);
			const recent = seen.slice(-5).reverse();
			if (!recent.length) {
				elAlarms.innerHTML =
					'<p class="rp-alarms-idle">No component has crossed its own baseline.</p>';
				return;
			}
			const rows = recent.map((c, i) => `
				<p class="rp-alarm${i === 0 ? ' is-latest' : ''}">
					<span class="rp-alarm-step">${num(c.step)}</span>
					<span class="rp-alarm-node">stage ${c.node}</span>
					<span class="rp-alarm-diag rp-alarm-diag--${c.diagnosis}">${DIAGNOSIS[c.diagnosis] ?? c.diagnosis}</span>
					<span class="rp-alarm-cap">${c.ceiling != null ? `≤ ${c.ceiling.toFixed(2)} w` : ''}</span>
				</p>`).join('');
			elAlarms.innerHTML =
				`<p class="rp-alarms-count">${seen.length} written so far</p>${rows}`;
		}

		function renderBeat(epoch: number) {
			const ev = bundle?.event;
			if (!ev || epoch < ev.epoch) { elBeat.hidden = true; return; }
			const named = ev.detected_nodes?.length
				? ev.detected_nodes.map((n) => `stage ${n}`).join(' and ')
				: null;
			if (ev.detected_step != null && ev.step_confirmed && ev.detected_lag_steps != null) {
				const lag = ev.detected_lag_steps;
				elBeat.innerHTML =
					`<strong>${ev.label}</strong> at step ${num(ev.step)}.` +
					(named ? ` Named at step ${num(ev.detected_step)} — ${lag === 1 ? 'one step' : `${num(lag)} steps`} later, on ${named}.` : '');
			} else {
				elBeat.innerHTML = `<strong>${ev.label}</strong> at epoch ${ev.epoch}.` +
					(named ? ` Named within one epoch, on ${named}.` : '');
			}
			elBeat.hidden = false;
		}

		/** `upto` limits the rows to the corrections written by that epoch, so the strip shows
		 *  the record AS IT IS BEING WRITTEN rather than jumping from empty to complete. */
		function renderRecord(upto?: number) {
			if (!bundle) return;
			const rec = bundle.record as any;
			const c = bundle.conditions;
			q('[data-record-sub]')!.textContent =
				`${c.run_id_ormas} · seed ${c.seed} · ${c.dataset} · generated during training`;

			const m = rec.mission ?? {}, shock = rec.shock ?? null, rcv = rec.recovery ?? null;
			// THREE GROUPS, NOT NINE LOOSE CELLS. As a flat auto-fit grid these wrapped into
			// ragged rows — "epochs with corrections" took two lines while its neighbours took
			// one — and nine equal-weight labels in a line say nothing about which belong
			// together. They are the run, the event, and the recovery, which is the order the
			// thing actually happened in, and a run with no event simply has no middle group.
			//
			// Values carry a unit or a qualifier as a separate span rather than inside the
			// number, so the figures align down the column instead of each one starting
			// wherever its prose ended.
			type Cell = [string, string, string?];
			const groups: [string, Cell[]][] = [
				['The run', [
					['Epochs', String(m.epochs ?? '—')],
					['Peak accuracy', m.peak != null ? pct(m.peak) : '—', m.peak != null ? `at epoch ${m.peak_epoch}` : ''],
					['Final accuracy', m.final != null ? pct(m.final) : '—'],
					['Corrections', String(m.corrections ?? '—'),
						m.epochs_with_corrections != null ? `across ${m.epochs_with_corrections} epochs` : ''],
				]],
			];
			if (shock) {
				groups.push(['The event', [
					['At epoch', String(shock.epoch)],
					['Accuracy before', pct(shock.pre)],
					['Accuracy after', pct(shock.post), `a fall of ${(shock.severity_pp ?? 0).toFixed(2)} points`],
				]]);
			}
			if (rcv) {
				groups.push(['The recovery', [
					['Recovered to', pct(rcv.to), `by epoch ${rcv.by_epoch}`],
					['Of what was lost', `${rcv.reclaimed_pct}%`],
				]]);
			}
			q('[data-record-grid]')!.innerHTML = groups.map(([name, cells]) => `
				<section class="rp-rec-group">
					<h5 class="rp-rec-group-name">${name}</h5>
					<dl class="rp-rec-cells">${cells.map(([k, v, note]) => `
						<div class="rp-record-cell">
							<dt>${k}</dt>
							<dd>${v}${note ? `<span class="rp-rec-note">${note}</span>` : ''}</dd>
						</div>`).join('')}
					</dl>
				</section>`).join('');

			const rows = upto == null
				? (bundle.corrections as Correction[])
				: (bundle.corrections as Correction[]).filter((x) => (x.epoch ?? 0) <= upto);
			q('[data-record-rows]')!.innerHTML = rows.map((x) => `
				<tr><td>${num(x.step)}</td><td>stage ${x.node}</td>
				<td>${DIAGNOSIS[x.diagnosis] ?? x.diagnosis}</td>
				<td>${x.magnitude != null ? x.magnitude.toFixed(4) : '—'}</td>
				<td>${x.ceiling != null ? x.ceiling.toFixed(2) : '—'}</td></tr>`).join('');
		}

		// ── TABS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
		//
		// One panel visible at a time is what gets the component under the fold line. The
		// panels use the `hidden` attribute and carry NO display rule of their own, so
		// reset.css's `[hidden] { display: none !important }` is what hides them — the same
		// trap that shipped an always-visible guide card is avoided by simply not setting
		// display here. check.py's fourth audit fails the build if anyone adds one.
		const tabs = [...root.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-tab]')];
		const panels = [...root.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-panel]')];

		// IN APP MODE NOTHING IS HIDDEN. The four readouts are cells of one face and are all
		// on screen together, so selecting is only ever about which one the guided read is
		// pointing at. Outside app mode the component shares a page and still tabs.
		const allVisible = root.classList.contains('rp--app');
		if (allVisible) {
			for (const el of panels) el.hidden = false;
		}

		function selectTab(name: string, focus = false) {
			for (const b of tabs) {
				const on = b.dataset.tab === name;
				b.setAttribute('aria-selected', String(on));
				b.tabIndex = on ? 0 : -1;
				if (on && focus) b.focus();
			}
			if (!allVisible) {
				for (const el of panels) el.hidden = el.dataset.panel !== name;
			}


			// A canvas in a hidden panel has no box, so it sizes to zero and draws nothing.
			// Sizing on reveal is not an optimisation here; without it the accuracy tab is
			// blank the first time it is opened.
			if (name === 'accuracy' && !allVisible) {
				sizeCanvas(canvas);
				if (engine) render(engine.epoch);
			}
		}

		for (const b of tabs) {
			b.addEventListener('click', () => selectTab(b.dataset.tab!));
		}
		// Arrow keys move between tabs, which is what a tablist is expected to do.
		root.querySelector('.rp-tablist')?.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
			const k = (e as KeyboardEvent).key;
			const i = tabs.findIndex((b) => b.getAttribute('aria-selected') === 'true');
			if (k !== 'ArrowRight' && k !== 'ArrowLeft' && k !== 'Home' && k !== 'End') return;
			e.preventDefault();
			const next = k === 'Home' ? 0
				: k === 'End' ? tabs.length - 1
				: (i + (k === 'ArrowRight' ? 1 : tabs.length - 1)) % tabs.length;
			selectTab(tabs[next].dataset.tab!, true);
		});

		// ── THE RAIL DRIVES THE SAME STATE ──────────────────────────────────────
		//
		// In app mode the rail replaces the <select> and the tablist, which are removed from
		// the layout rather than hidden and mirrored. The rail calls the same functions, so
		// there is one path into scenario and panel and nothing to keep in sync.
		const railScenario = [...root.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-rail-scenario]')];

		for (const b of railScenario) {
			b.addEventListener('click', () => {
				if (select) { select.value = b.dataset.railScenario!; }
				for (const o of railScenario) o.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(o === b));
				load(b.dataset.railScenario!);
			});
		}



		/** The four cards at the top of the working area, live with the epoch. A metric strip
		 *  whose numbers do not move while the run plays is decoration. */
		function paintMetrics(epoch: number) {
			if (!bundle) return;
			const e = Math.round(epoch);
			const o = bundle.series.ormas.accuracy[e];
			const b = bundle.series.baseline.accuracy[e];
			const set = (sel: string, v: string) => {
				const el = root.querySelector(sel);
				if (el) el.textContent = v;
			};
			set('[data-metric-sealed]', b == null ? '—' : `${(b * 100).toFixed(1)}%`);
			set('[data-metric-open]', o == null ? '—' : `${(o * 100).toFixed(1)}%`);
			set('[data-metric-corrections]',
				String(bundle.corrections.filter((c) => (c.epoch ?? 0) <= e).length));

			const ev = bundle.event;
			const lag = ev?.detected_lag_steps;
			const named = ev != null && e >= ev.epoch;
			set('[data-metric-lag]', !ev ? 'n/a' : !named ? '—' : lag == null ? 'same epoch' : `${lag} step${lag === 1 ? '' : 's'}`);
			const note = root.querySelector('[data-metric-lag-note]');
			if (note) {
				note.textContent = !ev
					? 'no single event in this run'
					: !named ? 'nothing has happened yet' : 'after the event';
			}
		}

		function showRecord() { selectTab('record'); renderRecord(); }

		function setPlayLabel() {
			const on = engine?.isPlaying ?? false;
			lblPlay.textContent = on ? 'Pause' : 'Play';
			btnPlay.setAttribute('aria-label', on ? 'Pause' : 'Play');
			root.classList.toggle('is-running', on);
		}

		// ─── GUIDED READ ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
		//
		// It DRIVES the engine rather than sitting on top of it. Next resumes playback and the
		// frame callback stops the run when it reaches the next stop's epoch, so the reader
		// watches the run arrive at each beat instead of being teleported between freeze-frames.
		// The run is the argument; a slideshow of its stills is not.
		let guide: GuideStep[] = [];
		let guideAt = -1;          // -1 = not running
		let guideWaiting = -1;     // epoch the engine is currently travelling towards
		let travelFrom = -1;       // epoch the current travel started from, for the rail fill

		function guideRunning() { return guideAt >= 0; }

		// EVERY LOOKUP INSIDE THE DOCK GOES THROUGH gq, NEVER q.
		//
		// q() is bound to `root`, and the dock is re-parented to <body> while a guided read is
		// running — `.rp` has container-type: inline-size, so a fixed child of it is fixed to
		// the component rather than the viewport and has to leave. The first version of this
		// function used q() anyway. Every lookup returned null, the non-null assertion is a
		// compile-time claim and not a runtime guard, and the first assignment threw. The
		// component dimmed and no dock ever appeared: a blank grey screen with no way out.
		const gq = <T extends HTMLElement>(s: string) => elGuide.querySelector(s) as T | null;

		function paintGuide() {
			const step = guide[guideAt];
			if (!step) return;

			const count = gq('[data-guide-count]');
			if (count) count.textContent = `${guideAt + 1} / ${guide.length}`;
			const where = gq('[data-guide-where]');
			if (where) where.textContent = step.eyebrow ? ` · ${step.eyebrow}` : '';
			const title = gq('[data-guide-title]');
			if (title) title.textContent = step.title;
			const body = gq('[data-guide-body]');
			if (body) body.textContent = step.body;

			// The rail is the orientation device: every stop visible at once, the current one
			// filled, the rest reachable. Dots gave a reader no idea what was coming or how to
			// get back to something they had already passed.
			const rail = gq('[data-guide-rail]');
			if (rail) {
				rail.replaceChildren(...guide.map((s, i) => {
					const seg = document.createElement('button');
					seg.type = 'button';
					seg.className = 'rp-guide-seg';
					seg.dataset.seg = String(i);
					seg.setAttribute('aria-current', String(i === guideAt));
					seg.setAttribute('aria-label', `Stop ${i + 1} of ${guide.length}: ${s.title}`);
					if (i < guideAt) seg.dataset.done = 'true';
					return seg;
				}));
			}

			const back = gq<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-guide-back]');
			if (back) back.disabled = guideAt === 0;
			const next = gq<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-guide-next]');
			if (next) next.textContent = guideAt === guide.length - 1 ? 'Finish' : 'Next';

			// Highlight by ADDITION. The previous version dropped everything to 0.3, which made
			// a working instrument look disabled rather than guided. The rest now recedes only
			// slightly; the lit panel is raised by an outline and a lift.
			for (const el of root.querySelectorAll('.is-lit')) el.classList.remove('is-lit');
			const lit = root.querySelector(step.target);

			// OPEN THE TAB THE TARGET LIVES IN, FIRST. Three of the four stops point at
			// something that is now inside a tab panel, and a panel that is not selected is
			// `hidden` — so the guide was lighting an element with no box and telling the
			// reader to look at nothing. Caught by the browser test at stop 4 on every
			// viewport: "the highlighted panel is visible" failed the moment the panels
			// became tabs. Derived from the DOM rather than a second lookup table, so a stop
			// that points at a new panel needs no change here.
			const owner = lit?.closest<HTMLElement>('[data-panel]');
			if (owner && owner.hidden) selectTab(owner.dataset.panel!);

			lit?.classList.add('is-lit');

			// The dock does not move; the panel comes to the reader.
			elGuide.hidden = false;
			if (lit) scrollToBand(lit);
		}

		function guideStop() {
			guideAt = -1;
			guideWaiting = -1;
			travelFrom = -1;
			elGuide.classList.remove('is-travelling');
			elGuide.style.removeProperty('--travel');
			elGuide.hidden = true;
			// Back where the markup put it, so a client-side navigation cannot leave a
			// detached dock on the body of the next page.
			if (elGuide.parentElement !== root) root.appendChild(elGuide);
			root.classList.remove('is-guided');
			for (const el of root.querySelectorAll('.is-lit')) el.classList.remove('is-lit');
			engine?.pause();
			setPlayLabel();
		}

		/** Travel to a stop. Under reduced motion the run does not play between stops —
		 *  it seeks, because the motion IS the thing being suppressed. */
		/** Centre the lit panel in the band between the fixed nav and the dock.
		 *
		 *  scrollIntoView({block:'center'}) centres against the VIEWPORT, which knows nothing
		 *  about either fixture — measured in a browser, it left the verdict table at y=1093
		 *  in a 900px window, entirely below the fold, on the one stop whose whole point is
		 *  that the table changes. This computes the free band and centres in that. A panel
		 *  taller than the band is aligned to its top instead, because centring something
		 *  that cannot fit hides both of its ends. */
		function scrollToBand(lit: Element) {
			const navH = document.querySelector('.nav')?.getBoundingClientRect().height ?? 0;
			const dockH = elGuide.hidden ? 0 : elGuide.getBoundingClientRect().height;
			const bandTop = navH + 16;
			const band = window.innerHeight - dockH - 16 - bandTop;
			const r = lit.getBoundingClientRect();
			const offset = r.height > band ? bandTop : bandTop + (band - r.height) / 2;
			window.scrollTo({
				top: Math.max(0, r.top + window.scrollY - offset),
				behavior: reduced ? 'auto' : 'smooth',
			});
		}

		/** Travel to a stop.
		 *
		 *  THE DOCK IS NEVER HIDDEN. The first version hid it while the run travelled, and
		 *  travelling from epoch 101 to 199 takes several seconds — so the reader got a dimmed
		 *  component and no controls at all, which is indistinguishable from the thing having
		 *  crashed. The destination stop is painted immediately, the dock says it is running,
		 *  the rail segment fills as the epochs pass, and pressing Next again arrives at once.
		 *
		 *  `instant` skips the travel: used by the rail, by Back, and by a second Next. */
		function guideGoTo(i: number, instant = false) {
			if (!engine || i < 0 || i >= guide.length) return guideStop();
			const target = guide[i].epoch;
			const here = Math.round(Number(elScrub.value));
			guideAt = i;

			if (reduced || instant || target <= here) {
				engine.seekEpoch(target);
				guideWaiting = -1;
				travelFrom = -1;
				elGuide.classList.remove('is-travelling');
				paintGuide();
				setPlayLabel();
				return;
			}

			guideWaiting = target;
			travelFrom = here;
			paintGuide();                       // destination copy is up before the travel starts
			elGuide.classList.add('is-travelling');
			engine.play();
			setPlayLabel();
		}

		function guideStart() {
			if (!engine || !bundle) return;
			guide = buildGuide(bundle);
			if (guide.length === 0) return;
			// OUT OF THE COMPONENT. `.rp` sets container-type: inline-size, which applies
			// layout containment and makes it the containing block for fixed-position
			// descendants — the dock would be fixed to the component, not the viewport.
			// Scoping is attribute-based, so the styles travel with the element.
			document.body.appendChild(elGuide);
			root.classList.add('is-guided');
			selectTab('answers');
			engine.setRate(4);          // the gaps between stops are long at 1x
			for (const b of root.querySelectorAll('[data-rate]')) {
				b.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(b.getAttribute('data-rate') === '4'));
			}
			engine.seekEpoch(0);
			guideAt = 0;
			guideWaiting = -1;
			paintGuide();
			setPlayLabel();
			// No scrollIntoView on the dock: it is position:fixed, so there is nothing to scroll to.
		}

		root.querySelector('[data-retry]')?.addEventListener('click', () => {
			load(select?.value ?? root.dataset.scenario ?? 'dead-layer-lesion');
		});

		btnGuide.addEventListener('click', () => (guideRunning() ? guideStop() : guideStart()));
		gq('[data-guide-skip]')!.addEventListener('click', guideStop);
		gq('[data-guide-next]')!.addEventListener('click', () => {
			// Pressed while the run is still travelling: arrive now rather than queueing.
			if (guideWaiting >= 0) { guideGoTo(guideAt, true); return; }
			if (guideAt >= guide.length - 1) { guideStop(); showRecord(); return; }
			guideGoTo(guideAt + 1);
		});
		gq('[data-guide-back]')!.addEventListener('click', () => {
			if (guideAt > 0) guideGoTo(guideAt - 1, true);
		});
		// Delegated: the rail's segments are rebuilt on every paint.
		elGuide.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
			const seg = (e.target as HTMLElement)?.closest?.('[data-seg]') as HTMLElement | null;
			if (seg) guideGoTo(Number(seg.dataset.seg), true);
		});
		// ESCAPE LEAVES IT — ON document, NOT ON root.
		//
		// A keydown listener on the component only hears keys pressed while focus is inside
		// it, and the dock is re-parented to <body> while the guide runs. Nothing in the
		// component has focus when a reader arrives and the guide has opened itself, so the
		// key went to <body> and was never heard. The dock prints "esc" on its Skip button;
		// the key has to actually do it.
		const onEsc = (e: KeyboardEvent) => {
			if (e.key === 'Escape' && guideRunning()) { e.preventDefault(); guideStop(); }
		};
		document.addEventListener('keydown', onEsc);
		teardown.push(() => document.removeEventListener('keydown', onEsc));

		/** Say so, with a way out. `reason` null clears it. */
		function setFailed(reason: string | null) {
			const on = reason !== null;
			root.classList.toggle('is-failed', on);
			const msg = on
				? `The archived run could not be loaded — ${reason}.`
				: 'Loading the archived run…';
			elRun.textContent = msg;
			const appRun = root.querySelector('[data-conditions-app]');
			if (appRun) appRun.textContent = msg;
			const box = root.querySelector('[data-failed]');
			if (box) (box as HTMLElement).hidden = !on;
		}

		async function load(key: string) {
			const embedded = (window as unknown as {
				__REPLAY_BUNDLES__?: Record<string, Bundle>;
			}).__REPLAY_BUNDLES__;
			if (embedded && embedded[key]) {
				bundle = embedded[key];
			} else {
				// A THROWN FETCH USED TO TAKE THE WHOLE LOAD WITH IT. Only `!res.ok` was
				// handled, so a network failure, a blocked script or an interception — a
				// hosting challenge page, say — rejected the promise unhandled and the
				// console sat on "Loading the archived run…" for ever. Everything around it
				// still rendered, which is worse than an error: a dark screen with a
				// scrubber, empty readouts and no explanation reads as the product being
				// broken rather than a file not arriving.
				setFailed(null);
				try {
					const res = await fetch(`/black-box/runs/${key}.json`);
					if (!res.ok) { setFailed(`the server answered ${res.status}`); return; }
					bundle = await res.json();
				} catch (err) {
					setFailed(err instanceof Error ? err.message : 'the request did not complete');
					return;
				}
			}
			if (!bundle) return;
			setFailed(null);

			engine?.destroy();
			engine = new ReplayEngine(bundle);
			engine.onFrame = (e) => {
				render(e);
				// Travelling: fill the live rail segment so the wait reads as motion rather
				// than as the thing having stopped responding.
				if (guideWaiting >= 0) {
					const span = Math.max(1, guideWaiting - travelFrom);
					const pct = Math.max(0, Math.min(1, (e - travelFrom) / span));
					elGuide.style.setProperty('--travel', `${(pct * 100).toFixed(1)}%`);
					const live = elGuide.querySelector('[data-guide-live]');
					if (live) live.textContent = `running · epoch ${Math.round(e)}`;
				}
				// Arrive, hold, and settle the card.
				if (guideWaiting >= 0 && e >= guideWaiting) {
					guideWaiting = -1;
					travelFrom = -1;
					engine?.pause();
					engine?.seekEpoch(guide[guideAt].epoch);
					elGuide.classList.remove('is-travelling');
					elGuide.style.removeProperty('--travel');
					setPlayLabel();
					paintGuide();
				}
			};
			engine.onEnd = () => {
				setPlayLabel();
				// An ambient embed loops; the run holds on its final frame for a
				// beat first, so the recovered figure and the finished record
				// register before it starts over.
				if (root.dataset.loop === 'true' && !reduced) {
					clearTimeout(loopTimer);
					loopTimer = window.setTimeout(() => {
						if (!inView || !engine) return;
						engine.seekEpoch(0);
						engine.play();
						setPlayLabel();
					}, 2200);
					return;
				}
				showRecord();
			};

			// The looping homepage embed is ambient: it plays itself and must not invite a
			// reader into a modal sequence over the top of it. The button is removed there
			// rather than hidden, so nothing can tab to it.
			if (root.dataset.loop === 'true') btnGuide.remove();

			// A scenario change abandons any guide in progress — its stops are built from the
			// bundle that was loaded, and half of them would point at epochs in the old run.
			if (guideRunning()) guideStop();

			anaBase.load(bundle);
			anaOrmas.load(bundle);

			const c = bundle.conditions;
			elRun.textContent =
				`${c.dataset} · seed ${c.seed} · run ${c.archive_index} · ${c.run_id_ormas}`;
			const appRun = root.querySelector('[data-conditions-app]');
			if (appRun) appRun.textContent = elRun.textContent;
			if (elTitle) elTitle.textContent = bundle.title;

			// A run with no event has a different shape of story and the chrome
			// has to say so, or the reader waits for a catastrophe that never
			// comes and concludes the demo is broken.
			const hasEvent = bundle.event != null;
			btnEvent.textContent = hasEvent ? 'Replay from the event' : 'Replay from the divergence';
			const nature = q('[data-nature]');
			if (nature) {
				nature.textContent = hasEvent
					? ''
					: 'Nothing is inflicted in this run. The corruption is in the data from the first step.';
			}

			elScrub.max = String(engine.lastEpoch);
			selectTab('answers');
			q('[data-diff-panel]')!.hidden = true;

			if (reduced || root.dataset.openRecord === 'true') {
				engine.toEnd();   // fires onEnd, which opens the record
			} else {
				engine.seekEpoch(0);
			}
			setPlayLabel();
			maybeAutoStart();   // the observer may already have fired
		}

		// TAKING A CONTROL ENDS THE GUIDE. Someone who reaches for the scrubber or the play
		// button has decided to drive; leaving a sequence half-open over the top of that is
		// the behaviour everyone hates about tours. It does not resume, and it does not ask.
		btnPlay.addEventListener('click', () => {
			clearTimeout(loopTimer);
			if (guideRunning()) guideStop();
			engine?.toggle();
			setPlayLabel();
		});
		btnEvent.addEventListener('click', () => {
			if (guideRunning()) guideStop();
			if (!engine || !bundle) return;
			if (bundle.event) {
				engine.replayFromEvent();
			} else {
				// Where the arms first part by more than a point and stay parted.
				const o = bundle.series.ormas.accuracy;
				const b = bundle.series.baseline.accuracy;
				let at = 0;
				for (let e = 1; e < o.length; e++) {
					if (o[e] - b[e] > 0.01) { at = e; break; }
				}
				engine.pause();
				engine.seekEpoch(Math.max(0, at - 5));
				engine.play();
			}
			setPlayLabel();
		});
		elScrub.addEventListener('input', () => {
			clearTimeout(loopTimer);
			if (guideRunning()) guideStop();
			engine?.pause(); engine?.seekEpoch(Number(elScrub.value)); setPlayLabel();
		});
		root.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-rate]').forEach((btn) => {
			btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
				engine?.setRate(Number(btn.dataset.rate));
				root.querySelectorAll('[data-rate]').forEach((o) =>
					o.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(o === btn)));
			});
		});
		select?.addEventListener('change', () => {
			root.dataset.openRecord = 'false';
			autoStarted = false;   // a chosen scenario should run, not wait
			load(select.value);
			const u = new URL(location.href);
			u.searchParams.set('scenario', select.value);
			history.replaceState(null, '', u);
		});

		q<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-download]')?.addEventListener('click', () => {
			if (!bundle) return;
			const payload = {
				note: 'Modification record generated during training. Replayed from the '
					+ 'archived run named below; regenerable from its seed.',
				conditions: bundle.conditions, event: bundle.event, record: bundle.record,
			};
			const blob = new Blob([JSON.stringify(payload, null, 2)], { type: 'application/json' });
			const a = document.createElement('a');
			a.href = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
			a.download = `ormas-record-${bundle.key}-${bundle.conditions.run_id_ormas}.json`;
			a.click();
			URL.revokeObjectURL(a.href);
		});

		q<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-diff]')?.addEventListener('click', () => {
			const panel = q('[data-diff-panel]')!;
			if (!bundle || !bundle.event) return;
			if (!panel.hidden) { panel.hidden = true; return; }
			const before = bundle.health.find((f) => f.epoch === bundle!.event!.epoch - 1);
			const after = bundle.health[bundle.health.length - 1];
			if (!before || !after) return;
			panel.innerHTML = `
				<p class="rp-diff-label">Component state, epoch ${before.epoch} against epoch ${after.epoch}</p>
				<table class="rp-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Component</th>
				<th scope="col">Weight norm before</th><th scope="col">After</th><th scope="col">Change</th></tr></thead>
				<tbody>${before.nodes.map((bn) => {
					const an = after.nodes.find((z) => z.id === bn.id);
					const bw = bn.weight_norm ?? 0, aw = an?.weight_norm ?? 0, d = aw - bw;
					return `<tr><td>stage ${bn.id}</td><td>${bw.toFixed(2)}</td><td>${aw.toFixed(2)}</td><td>${d >= 0 ? '+' : ''}${d.toFixed(2)}</td></tr>`;
				}).join('')}</tbody></table>`;
			panel.hidden = false;
		});

		// The replay starts itself the first time it is properly on screen. A demo
		// that waits to be pressed is a demo most readers scroll past: on the home
		// page especially, this section IS the argument and the play button is one
		// more thing to not do.
		//
		// Once only, so scrolling back up does not restart a run mid-way and lose
		// the reader's place; never under reduced motion; and never in the mode
		// that opens straight on the record, where the run is already over.
		// The observer fires once on observe(), which can be BEFORE the bundle has
		// finished loading and the engine exists. So intersection is recorded as
		// state and the decision is taken from two places — whichever of the two
		// happens second wins, and neither can miss the other.
		let autoStarted = false;
		let inView = false;
		let loopTimer = 0;

		// FIRST VISIT GETS THE GUIDED READ, ONCE.
		//
		// Nobody clicks a button they do not know is there, so on the page whose subject is
		// this component the guided read opens itself the first time the component is
		// scrolled to. Once per browser: the flag is written the moment it fires, so a second
		// visit gets the plain autoplay and is never walked through it again.
		//
		// localStorage is wrapped both ways. It throws in a private window and in a browser
		// with site data blocked, and an exception here would take out the autostart with it.
		// A reader whose storage is unavailable gets the guide every visit, which is the safe
		// direction to fail in.
		const GUIDE_SEEN = 'oxiedo.blackbox.guideSeen';
		function guideAlreadySeen() {
			try { return localStorage.getItem(GUIDE_SEEN) === '1'; } catch { return false; }
		}
		function rememberGuideSeen() {
			try { localStorage.setItem(GUIDE_SEEN, '1'); } catch { /* storage unavailable */ }
		}

		// THE COLD OPEN INTERLOCK. /black-box plays a title sequence over the loaded page, and
		// the console is in view BEHIND it — the IntersectionObserver cannot tell the
		// difference. Without this the guided read would open behind the curtain, spend its
		// once-per-browser showing on nobody, and the reader would surface into a dock
		// halfway through a sentence.
		//   'running' — hold everything; the curtain is up.
		//   'done'    — the reader has just been walked through the whole argument, so let
		//               autoplay through but skip the guide. One onboarding at a time is the
		//               same rule that put a single prose band on this screen instead of two.
		// The flag is never set on any other page, so nothing here changes elsewhere.
		const coldOpen = () => document.documentElement.getAttribute('data-coldopen');

		function maybeAutoStart() {
			if (autoStarted) return;
			if (coldOpen() === 'running') return;   // before autoStarted, so it can retry after
			if (root.dataset.openRecord === 'true') return;
			if (!inView || !engine || engine.isPlaying) return;
			autoStarted = true;

			// THE GUIDED READ IS NOT SUPPRESSED BY REDUCED MOTION. It was, because it sat
			// behind the same early return as autoplay — and a reader with "reduce motion"
			// set on their system therefore never saw it at all and had to find the button,
			// which is exactly what was reported. Reduced motion is a request to stop things
			// moving, not a request to be told less: guideGoTo already seeks between stops
			// instead of playing when it is set, so the guide reads as a series of stills.
			// Autoplay stays suppressed, because that IS motion nobody asked for.
			if (root.dataset.guideMode === 'auto' && coldOpen() !== 'done'
				&& !guideAlreadySeen() && !guideRunning()) {
				guideStart();
				// WRITTEN AFTER, NOT BEFORE. The flag used to be set first, so a start that
				// bailed — no bundle yet, a scenario with no stops — burned the single
				// showing and the reader never got it on any later visit either.
				if (guideRunning()) rememberGuideSeen();
				return;
			}

			if (reduced) return;
			engine.seekEpoch(0);
			engine.play();
			setPlayLabel();
		}

		// The canvas is visible from the start in app mode, but only after the grid has laid
		// out — sizing it in the same frame gives a zero-width box and an empty chart.
		if (allVisible) {
			requestAnimationFrame(() => {
				sizeCanvas(canvas);
				if (engine) render(engine.epoch);
			});
		}

		const io = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
			inView = entries[0]?.isIntersecting ?? true;
			if (!inView) { clearTimeout(loopTimer); engine?.pause(); setPlayLabel(); return; }
			maybeAutoStart();
		}, { threshold: 0.4 });
		io.observe(root);

		// The curtain has come down: take over.
		const onColdOpenDone = () => maybeAutoStart();
		document.addEventListener('coldopen:done', onColdOpenDone);

		const onVisibility = () => {
			if (document.hidden) { engine?.pause(); setPlayLabel(); }
		};
		document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', onVisibility);

		let rt = 0;
		const onResize = () => {
			clearTimeout(rt);
			rt = window.setTimeout(() => {
				theme = readTheme(root);
				sizeCanvas(canvas);
				if (engine) render(engine.epoch);
			}, 120);
		};
		window.addEventListener('resize', onResize);

		teardown.push(() => {
			clearTimeout(loopTimer);
			// The dock may be parented to <body>; ClientRouter swaps the page under it and
			// would otherwise strand a fixed panel over the next route.
			if (guideRunning()) guideStop();
			elGuide.remove();
			engine?.destroy(); engine = null;
			anaBase.destroy(); anaOrmas.destroy();
			io.disconnect();
			window.removeEventListener('resize', onResize);
			document.removeEventListener('visibilitychange', onVisibility);
			document.removeEventListener('coldopen:done', onColdOpenDone);
			clearTimeout(rt);
			delete root.dataset.replayReady;
		});

		const url = new URL(location.href);
		const wanted = url.searchParams.get('scenario');
		const initial = (wanted && select
			&& Array.from(select.options).some((o) => o.value === wanted))
			? wanted : (root.dataset.scenario || 'dead-layer-lesion');
		if (select) select.value = initial;
		load(initial);
	}

	function initAll() {
		document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-replay]').forEach((element) => {
			try { init(element); } catch (err) { console.error('[replay] init failed', err); }
		});
	}

	document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', initAll);
	document.addEventListener('astro:before-swap', () => {
		while (teardown.length) teardown.pop()?.();
	});
	if (document.readyState !== 'loading') initAll();
	else document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', initAll);
</script>

<style>
	/* THE REPLAY INSTRUMENT, as a scientific plate.
	   REPALETTED 2026-09-17. The first version ran on --bg-dark, on the theory
	   that telemetry reads as emitted signal and every instrument console is
	   dark. That reference was wrong twice over. A single dark slab on a cream
	   editorial site reads as a foreign object — an embedded player rather than
	   part of the document — and near-black with one amber accent is the exact
	   generic treatment this site's design notes warn against.
	   The right reference is the printed plate: an anatomical drawing, an
	   engineering section, a spectrogram. All light-ground, and so are the
	   technical account's own figures, where the ORMAS line is already this
	   amber. A light plate also survives being screenshotted into a deck or a
	   paper, which is most of what this asset is for.
	   No new palette: every value below is a token the site already ships. */

	.rp {
		--plate: var(--bg-surface);
		--plate-sunk: var(--bg-base);
		--rule: var(--border-hairline);
		--fg: var(--text-primary);
		--fg-mid: var(--text-secondary);
		--fg-dim: var(--text-muted);
		--ormas: var(--chart-ormas);
		--base: var(--chart-baseline);
		--warn: var(--chart-warning);
		--ormas-text: var(--ormas-ink);
		--mono: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;

		background: var(--plate);
		color: var(--fg);
		border: 1px solid var(--rule);
		border-radius: 4px;
		overflow: hidden;
		container-type: inline-size;
	}

	/* ── chrome ───────────────────────────────────────────────── */

	.rp-bar {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		gap: var(--space-sm) var(--space-lg);
		align-items: baseline;
		justify-content: space-between;
		padding: var(--space-md) var(--space-lg);
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
	}

	.rp-bar-left { display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: var(--space-md); min-width: 0; }
	.rp-pick { display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: var(--space-sm); }

	.rp-pick-label, .rp-chart-label {
		font-size: var(--text-eyebrow);
		letter-spacing: 0.09em;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		color: var(--fg-dim);
	}

	.rp-select {
		font: inherit;
		font-size: var(--text-small);
		color: var(--fg);
		background: var(--plate);
		border: 1px solid var(--rule);
		border-radius: 3px;
		padding: 4px 8px;
	}

	.rp-title { margin: 0; font-size: var(--text-small); font-weight: 600; }

	/* Conditions stay in the chrome at every frame: screenshots travel without
	   their context, and a figure loose on a timeline with no conditions reads
	   as an overclaim. */
	.rp-run { margin: 0; font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11px; color: var(--fg-dim); }

	/* ── the anatomy band: the hero ───────────────────────────── */

	.rp-stageband { padding: 0; }
	.rp-readout, .rp-controls { padding-inline: var(--space-lg); }

	/* Folded into the top bar when the controls moved up under the network:
	   it did not earn a 40px row of its own. */
	.rp-legend-item {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: var(--text-small);
		color: var(--fg-mid);
		min-width: 0;
	}

	.rp-panes {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
		border-block: 1px solid var(--rule);
	}

	.rp-pane {
		border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--rule) 32%, transparent);
		border-radius: 4px;
		/* Tight vertically: the anatomy inside is a fixed 300px, so every pixel of pane
		   padding is a pixel of component height, and the component has to clear the fold. */
		padding: 8px var(--space-md);
		min-width: 0;
		background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink-raised) 45%, transparent);
	}
	/* The open pane is lifted a half-step. The difference between the two is
	   the entire argument, so it gets exactly one unit of emphasis and no more. */
	.rp-pane--sealed { /* geometry identical to the open pane, by design */ }
	.rp-pane--open {
		background: var(--ink-raised);
		border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ormas) 28%, transparent);
	}

	.rp-anatomy { width: 100%; }

	/* ── SVG anatomy internals ────────────────────────────────── */

	.rp :global(.an-name) {
		font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.06em;
		fill: var(--fg); font-family: var(--font-sans, system-ui);
	}
	.rp :global(.an-badge) {
		font-size: 9px; letter-spacing: 0.14em; font-family: var(--mono);
		fill: var(--fg-dim);
	}
	.rp :global(.an-sub) { font-size: 9px; font-family: var(--mono); fill: var(--fg-dim); }
	.rp-pane--open :global(.an-badge) { fill: var(--ormas); }
	.rp :global(.an-sub) { font-size: 9px; font-family: var(--mono); fill: var(--fg-dim); }

	.rp :global(.an-input) {
		fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--base) 30%, transparent);
		stroke: var(--base); stroke-width: 1.1;
	}
	.rp :global(.an-output) {
		fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--fg) 18%, transparent);
		stroke: color-mix(in srgb, var(--fg) 45%, transparent); stroke-width: 1.1;
	}
	.rp :global(.an-io) { font-size: 9px; font-family: var(--mono); fill: var(--fg-dim); }
	.rp :global(.an-io-sub) { font-size: 8px; font-family: var(--mono); fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--fg) 34%, transparent); }

	/* ── the network itself ───────────────────────────────────── */

	/* Edges are recessive: they carry structure, not state. The units and the
	   signal carry state, and if the web competed with them the drawing would
	   read as decoration. */
	.rp :global(.an-edge) {
		stroke: color-mix(in srgb, var(--fg) 13%, transparent);
		stroke-width: 0.6;
		fill: none;
	}
	.rp-pane--open :global(.an-edge) { stroke: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ormas) 26%, transparent); }

	.rp :global(.an-spine) {
		stroke: color-mix(in srgb, var(--fg) 10%, transparent);
		stroke-width: 1;
	}

	/* A unit's fill is its stage's health, passed in as --health. Uniform across
	   the stage on purpose: the telemetry resolves to the stage, not the
	   channel. */
	.rp :global(.an-unit) {
		fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ormas) calc(var(--health, 0) * 100%), var(--plate));
		stroke: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ormas) 70%, transparent);
		stroke-width: 1.1;
		transition: fill 260ms var(--ease-out);
	}
	/* Sealed: the units are there, drawn at full strength, reporting nothing.
	   Hollow rather than dim — the stage is working, it is just not observable. */
	.rp :global(.an-unit.is-sealed) {
		fill: var(--plate);
		stroke: color-mix(in srgb, var(--fg) 34%, transparent);
	}
	.rp :global(.an-unit.is-destroyed) {
		fill: var(--plate);
		stroke: var(--warn);
		stroke-width: 1.6;
		stroke-dasharray: 2 1.6;
	}

	/* Health as a soft field around the column, so a healthy stage reads at a
	   glance without having to compare eleven small circles. */
	.rp :global(.an-halo) {
		fill: var(--ormas);
		transition: opacity 260ms var(--ease-out);
	}
	.rp :global(.an-footnote) {
		font-size: 7px; font-family: var(--mono); fill: var(--fg-dim);
	}

	/* A correction fired on this component — the working colour. Not the same
	   claim as destruction, which has its own outline: drawing both in the
	   warning colour made a stage that had merely self-corrected read as
	   wreckage. */
	.rp :global(.an-strike) { transition: opacity 160ms linear; }
	.rp :global(.an-strike rect) { fill: none; stroke: var(--ormas); stroke-width: 1.6; }
	.rp :global(.an-gone) {
		fill: none; stroke: var(--warn); stroke-width: 1.6;
		transition: opacity 200ms linear;
	}

	/* ── stage labels ─────────────────────────────────────────────
	   Every one of these needs an explicit font-size. SVG text with none
	   inherits the page's body size and is then multiplied by the viewBox
	   scale, which is how these once rendered at ~29px. */
	.rp :global(.an-stage-shape) {
		font-size: 10px; font-family: var(--mono); font-weight: 700; fill: var(--fg);
	}
	.rp :global(.an-stage-params) {
		font-size: 8px; font-family: var(--mono); fill: var(--fg-dim);
	}
	.rp :global(.an-stage-state) {
		font-size: 9px; font-family: var(--mono); fill: var(--fg-mid);
	}
	.rp :global(.an-stage-state.is-destroyed) { fill: var(--warn); font-weight: 700; }
	.rp :global(.an-stage-metric) {
		font-size: 8px; font-family: var(--mono); fill: var(--fg-dim);
	}

	/* THE SIGNAL. One pulse per unit per gap, staggered, travelling forward.
	   This is the single piece of motion that makes the drawing a network
	   rather than a diagram of one. */
	.rp :global(.an-flow) {
		stroke: var(--base);
		stroke-width: 2;
		stroke-linecap: round;
		fill: none;
		stroke-dasharray: 5 999;
		stroke-dashoffset: 0;
		opacity: 0;
	}
	.rp-pane--open :global(.an-flow) { stroke: var(--ormas); }
	.rp.is-running :global(.an-flow) {
		opacity: 0.85;
		animation: rp-pulse 1.5s linear infinite;
	}
	@keyframes rp-pulse {
		0%   { stroke-dashoffset: 5;  opacity: 0; }
		12%  { opacity: 0.9; }
		88%  { opacity: 0.9; }
		100% { stroke-dashoffset: -100; opacity: 0; }
	}

	.rp :global(.an-tap) { stroke: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ormas) 50%, transparent); stroke-width: 0.9; stroke-dasharray: 2 2; }
	.rp :global(.an-op) {
		fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ormas) 10%, transparent);
		stroke: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ormas) 62%, transparent);
		stroke-width: 0.7;
	}
	.rp :global(.an-op-label) { font-size: 6.5px; font-family: var(--mono); fill: var(--ormas); }
	.rp :global(.an-bottleneck) {
		fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ormas) 8%, transparent);
		stroke: var(--ormas); stroke-width: 0.9;
	}
	.rp :global(.an-bottleneck-label) { font-size: 7.5px; font-family: var(--mono); fill: var(--ormas); }
	.rp :global(.an-chain-caption) {
		font-size: 7.5px; font-family: var(--mono);
		letter-spacing: 0.1em; fill: var(--ormas);
	}
	.rp :global(.an-chain-caption--void) { fill: var(--fg-dim); }
	.rp :global(.an-name) { fill: var(--fg); }

	.rp :global(.an-void) {
		fill: none;
		stroke: color-mix(in srgb, var(--fg) 26%, transparent);
		stroke-width: 0.9; stroke-dasharray: 3 3;
	}
	.rp :global(.an-void-label) { font-size: 9px; font-family: var(--mono); fill: var(--fg-mid); }
	.rp :global(.an-void-sub) { font-size: 7.5px; font-family: var(--mono); fill: var(--fg-dim); }

	/* ── readout row ──────────────────────────────────────────── */

	/* SHORT VIEWPORTS. The console is built to clear the fold at 900px of window height, which
	   leaves 811px under the nav. At 800px there are only 711px and it does not fit — so on a
	   short window the network drawing gives up 60px and the chrome tightens, which closes
	   most of the gap. Not all of it: at 800px tall the component still runs about 45px past
	   the fold, and shrinking the diagram further would make its labels unreadable, which is
	   a worse trade than a short scroll.

	   Two things this rule needs that are easy to miss. The !important is against an inline
	   style: anatomy.ts sets svg.style.height = "300px" when it draws, so nothing in a
	   stylesheet reaches it otherwise. And the :global() is because that svg is created in
	   JavaScript and therefore carries no [data-astro-cid] — a bare `> svg` compiles to
	   `svg[data-astro-cid-…]` and matches nothing, which is exactly how this first shipped:
	   the padding trims applied, the diagram did not, and the component moved 4px. */
	@media (max-height: 880px) {
		.rp-anatomy > :global(svg) { height: 240px !important; }
		.rp-plain { padding-block: 8px; }
		.rp-orient { padding-block: 9px; }
		.rp-pane { padding-block: 6px; }
		.rp-panel { padding-block: 10px; }
	}

	/* ══ APPLICATION MODE ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
	   /black-box only. Specs follow the enterprise-dashboard conventions rather than being
	   invented: a 220px rail with 36px items, a metric strip of four cards with a 30px
	   primary number, 24px gutters, and density over whitespace — the reader of a console is
	   looking for data, not for air.

	   THE SCREEN IS calc(100dvh - nav). The site header stays, because nothing outside this
	   page changes, so the app gets what is left. Measured: app bar 48 + metrics 86 + panes
	   336 + controls 60 + panel 220 + gutters 44 = 794 against 811 available at 1440×900.

	   THE RAIL COLLAPSES BEFORE THE NETWORKS DO. anatomy.ts holds each network to MIN_W 560,
	   so the pair needs 1120px; a 220px rail plus padding takes the working area under that
	   at 1400px of viewport, well before a reader would expect a problem. Below 1400 the rail
	   is icons only, and the pane stacking is driven by a container query on the working
	   area's real width rather than the viewport's, so it cannot disagree with the rail. */
	/* ── THE CONSOLE IS DARK ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
	   The light plate was right when this was a figure printed inside an article. As a
	   screen it was a cream admin template, and it read as one.

	   Dark ground, amber telemetry, monospace precision is the established language for
	   instruments that report on a running system, and here it is not borrowed: the data IS
	   telemetry, the ORMAS arm is ALREADY drawn in amber on every chart, and the figures are
	   already set in a mono face. The console is the palette agreeing with the content.

	   IT IS A TOKEN SWAP AND NOTHING ELSE. render.ts reads grid, text, muted, ormas, baseline
	   and event off this element's custom properties, and anatomy.ts is styled by classes
	   inside this component — so redefining six variables turns the chart, both network
	   drawings, the ledger and the tables over together, with no second code path to keep in
	   step. Measured against the panel ground: text 15.58:1, secondary 8.14:1, amber 8.01:1,
	   baseline blue 6.98:1, the event red 5.26:1.

	   The document below stays cream. The boundary is deliberate — the instrument is dark and
	   the writing about it is not, which is how a reader knows which one they are in. */
	.rp--app {
		--plate: #141625;
		--plate-sunk: #0F111C;
		--rule: #2F3550;
		--fg: #F1EFE8;
		--fg-mid: #A9AEC4;
		/* #6E7492 measured 3.91:1 and this token carries the metric labels, the chart labels,
		   the table headers and the run string — all 10-12px, so the 4.5 floor applies to
		   every one of them. #828AA8 is 5.25:1 on the panel and 5.50:1 on the sunk ground. */
		--fg-dim: #828AA8;
		--ormas: #E0A233;
		--ormas-text: #E0A233;
		--base: #7FA3DC;
		--warn: #E0645F;
		--ink-raised: #1A1D2E;
		background: var(--plate);
		color: var(--fg);

		--appbar: 48px;
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		/* The CONSOLE is one screen — the bar plus the working area. The record strip below
		   is deliberately outside that height, so it is the first thing a reader meets on
		   scrolling rather than a quarter-panel squeezed into the fold. */
		height: auto;
		min-height: 0;
		border: 0;
		border-radius: 0;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
	}

	.rp-appbar {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: space-between;
		gap: var(--space-md);
		height: var(--appbar);
		padding: 0 var(--space-lg);
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
		background: var(--plate-sunk);
		flex: none;
	}
	/* NOTHING IN THE BAR WRAPS. The segmented control is wider than the four words it
	   replaced, and the mark and the breadcrumb broke onto second lines rather than giving
	   way — a two-line product bar in a one-line bar's height. Everything here is a single
	   line; the run identifier is the one that may be clipped, because it is the only item
	   whose tail is not load-bearing. */
	.rp-appbar-mark {
		margin: 0;
		font-family: var(--mono);
		font-size: 11px;
		letter-spacing: 0.16em;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		white-space: nowrap;
		flex: none;
		color: var(--fg);
	}
	.rp-appbar-mark span { margin: 0 8px; color: var(--ormas); }
	.rp-appbar-trail { margin-right: var(--space-lg); flex: none; }
	.rp-appbar-trail ol { white-space: nowrap; }

	/* ── THE SCENARIO SWITCH ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
	   These read as four words printed on the bar rather than as four things to press:
	   flat, unlit, the same ink as the label beside them. Nothing said they were controls.

	   Built as a proper segmented control instead — the pattern's job is to say "one of
	   these, and you may change it", and it does that with three signals rather than one:

	     a RECESSED TRACK, darker than the bar, so the group reads as a slot holding
	       something rather than as text sitting on a surface;
	     a RAISED SELECTED PILL, filled and shadowed, which is the one that looks pressed in
	       and therefore implies the others can be;
	     UNSELECTED ITEMS THAT ARE PROPERLY LIT — --fg-mid at 8.14:1, not --fg-dim — because
	       an option nobody can read is not an option.

	   A standing "SCENARIO" label names what is being switched. Hover lifts, active depresses
	   by a pixel, and focus-visible rings, so a pointer and a keyboard both get an answer. */
	.rp-opts-label {
		flex: none;
		white-space: nowrap;
		font-family: var(--mono);
		font-size: 10px;
		letter-spacing: 0.14em;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		color: var(--fg-dim);
		margin: 0 10px 0 0;
		align-self: center;
	}
	.rp-opts {
		display: flex;
		flex: none;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 2px;
		margin-right: auto;
		padding: 3px;
		background: color-mix(in oklab, #000 34%, var(--plate));
		border: 1px solid var(--rule);
		border-radius: 6px;
		box-shadow: inset 0 1px 2px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.35);
	}
	/* EVERY OPTION IS A BUTTON, not just the chosen one. With a transparent unselected state
	   only the amber pill read as pressable and the other three read as labels printed
	   beside it — so nobody knew the run could be changed. Each one now carries its own
	   raised surface and edge; the selection is which of four buttons is lit, which is a
	   difference in colour rather than in kind. */
	.rp-opt {
		font: inherit;
		font-family: var(--mono);
		font-size: 10.5px;
		font-weight: 600;
		letter-spacing: 0.07em;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		white-space: nowrap;
		padding: 5px 12px;
		border: 1px solid color-mix(in oklab, var(--fg) 26%, transparent);
		border-radius: 4px;
		/* A SOLID COLOUR UNDER THE GRADIENT. A bare linear-gradient sets background-image and
		   leaves background-color transparent, so anything reading the computed colour — a
		   contrast check, a browser with gradients disabled, a forced-colours mode — sees
		   straight through to whatever is behind. The check caught the selected option at
		   1.17:1 for exactly that reason while it rendered correctly. */
		background-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--fg) 10%, var(--plate));
		background-image: linear-gradient(
			to bottom,
			color-mix(in oklab, var(--fg) 13%, var(--plate)),
			color-mix(in oklab, var(--fg) 7%, var(--plate))
		);
		color: var(--fg);
		cursor: pointer;
		box-shadow: 0 1px 0 rgb(0 0 0 / 0.30), inset 0 1px 0 rgb(255 255 255 / 0.07);
		transition:
			background var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out),
			color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out),
			border-color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out),
			transform var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out);
	}
	.rp-opt:hover {
		color: var(--fg);
		border-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--ormas) 65%, transparent);
		background-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--fg) 16%, var(--plate));
		background-image: linear-gradient(
			to bottom,
			color-mix(in oklab, var(--fg) 20%, var(--plate)),
			color-mix(in oklab, var(--fg) 12%, var(--plate))
		);
	}
	.rp-opt:active { transform: translateY(1px); }
	.rp-opt:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--ormas); outline-offset: 1px; }
	.rp-opt[aria-pressed='true'] {
		background-color: var(--ormas);
		background-image: linear-gradient(
			to bottom,
			color-mix(in oklab, #fff 10%, var(--ormas)),
			var(--ormas)
		);
		border-color: color-mix(in oklab, #000 26%, var(--ormas));
		color: var(--plate);
		font-weight: 700;
		box-shadow: 0 1px 4px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.45), inset 0 1px 0 rgb(255 255 255 / 0.28);
	}
	.rp-opt[aria-pressed='true']:hover {
		background-color: var(--ormas);
		background-image: none;
		color: var(--plate);
		border-color: color-mix(in oklab, #000 26%, var(--ormas));
	}
	.rp-appbar-trail ol {
		display: flex;
		gap: 6px;
		list-style: none;
		margin: 0;
		padding: 0;
		font-family: var(--mono);
		font-size: 10px;
		letter-spacing: 0.1em;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		color: var(--fg-dim);
	}
	.rp-appbar-trail li + li::before { content: '/'; margin-right: 6px; color: var(--fg-dim); }
	.rp-appbar-trail a { color: var(--fg-dim); text-decoration: none; }
	.rp-appbar-trail a:hover { color: var(--fg); }
	.rp-appbar-trail [aria-current='page'] { color: var(--fg-mid); }
	.rp-appbar-run {
		margin: 0;
		flex: 1 1 auto;
		text-align: right;
		min-width: 0;
		font-family: var(--mono);
		font-size: 11px;
		color: var(--fg-dim);
		overflow: hidden;
		text-overflow: ellipsis;
		white-space: nowrap;
	}

	/* THE CONSOLE IS THE ONE-SCREEN BOX, and the shell takes what the bands above it left.
	   The shell used to carry `height: calc(100dvh - nav - appbar)`, which had two faults.
	   It could not survive a new band above it — the arithmetic lived in one place and the
	   markup in another. And --appbar is a lie below 900px, where .rp-appbar is given
	   `height: auto; flex-wrap: wrap` and becomes two rows: the subtraction stayed 48px and
	   the console ran a whole bar past the fold at exactly the widths least able to afford
	   it. Both faults are the same fault — a number guessed here instead of measured by the
	   browser. The console now owns the height; every band above the shell is flex:none and
	   the shell is flex:1, so the arithmetic is the layout engine's.

	   min-height 568px is the old floor kept intact: shell 520 + appbar 48. The stripe
	   replaced .rp-plain inside the shell rather than adding to it, so the working area
	   below that floor is unchanged. */
	.rp--app .rp-console {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		height: calc(100dvh - var(--nav-h, 89px));
		min-height: 568px;
	}

	.rp--app .rp-shell {
		display: flex;
		flex: 1 1 auto;
		min-height: 0;
	}

	.rp--app .rp-main {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		min-width: 0;
		min-height: 0;
		overflow: hidden;
	}

	/* THE METRIC STRIP. Four cards, a 30px primary number each, live with the epoch — the
	   number a reader looks at first has to be the one that is changing. */
	.rp-metrics {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
		gap: 1px;
		background: var(--rule);
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
		flex: none;
	}
	/* A READOUT, NOT CARDS. Four rounded boxes in a row is the admin-template signature. These
	   are cells of one instrument face, divided by the same hairline as everything else, with
	   the label above the figure the way a panel is silkscreened. */
	.rp-metric { background: var(--plate); padding: 9px var(--space-lg) 10px; min-width: 0; }
	.rp-metric-label {
		margin: 0;
		font-family: var(--mono);
		font-size: 10px;
		letter-spacing: 0.12em;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		color: var(--fg-dim);
	}
	.rp-metric-value {
		margin: 3px 0 0;
		font-family: var(--mono);
		font-size: 30px;
		line-height: 1.05;
		letter-spacing: -0.01em;
		font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
		font-feature-settings: 'zero' 1;
		color: var(--fg);
	}
	.rp-metric--open .rp-metric-value { color: var(--ormas-text); }
	.rp-metric-note { margin: 1px 0 0; font-size: 11px; color: var(--fg-dim); }

	/* In app mode the rail owns scenario and panel, so the duplicates are removed rather than
	   hidden-and-synced: two controls writing one piece of state is how they drift apart. */
	.rp--app .rp-bar,
	.rp--app .rp-tablist { display: none; }
	/* THE PANEL TAKES WHAT IS LEFT, rather than a fixed height. A console's working area is
	   whatever the window did not spend on chrome, and a number guessed here is a number that
	   is wrong on the next screen size — 214px was, and every panel scrolled inside itself.
	   Everything above it is flex:none and the panel is flex:1, so the arithmetic is the
	   browser's rather than mine.

	   The diagram gives up 60px in app mode to make that space exist. At 1440×900 there are
	   811px: appbar 48 + metrics 86 + plain 52 + panes 276 + controls 60 leaves 289 for the
	   panel, against the 299 the answer table wants — close enough that the last row is
	   reachable, and 240px is the same height the short-viewport rule already uses, so the
	   labels are known to hold at it. */
	.rp--app .rp-plain { flex: none; }
	/* .rp-panes must be told to grow. The stageband is a flex container here, and a grid
	   child of a flex parent sizes to its content unless it is given flex:1 — which is why
	   the two networks sat in the left two thirds with dead cream beside them. */
	.rp--app .rp-stageband { flex: none; display: flex; }
	.rp--app .rp-panes { flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; }
	.rp--app .rp-controls { flex: none; }
	/* ── FOUR PANELS, ALL OF THEM, ALL THE TIME ──────────────────────────────────
	   Tabs were the right answer while this was a block inside a page competing for height.
	   On a screen of its own they are the wrong one: the whole argument of this instrument
	   is a comparison, and a comparison you have to click between is not one. The four
	   readouts share the bottom band as cells of a single face, divided by the same hairline
	   as the metric strip above them.

	   Each panel keeps its own heading — with no tablist, the heading is the only thing
	   naming it — and scrolls inside itself if its content outruns the band. */
	.rp--app .rp-tabs {
		flex: 1 1 auto;
		min-height: 0;
		display: grid;
		/* NOT FOUR EQUAL CELLS. The answer table is the panel that carries the argument and
		   the only one a non-technical reader parses on sight — at an equal quarter its
		   question column wrapped to three lines. It takes the space the chart and the
		   ledger do not need. Measured at 1512px: 483 / 302 / 302 / 423. */
		/* Three, not four. The record left this row for a full-width strip below, which gives
		   the answer table the width its question column needs and the chart enough box to
		   draw two bands in without its labels colliding. */
		grid-template-columns: 1.5fr 1.25fr 1fr;
		gap: 1px;
		background: var(--rule);
		border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
	}
	/* THE CONTENT FILLS THE PANEL. Each readout stopped where its content stopped and left
	   93px, 93px and 139px of dead ground beneath it — measured — because the panels were
	   sized for the four-up row they were in before the record moved out. They are flex
	   columns now and the thing inside each one grows. */
	.rp--app .rp-panel {
		height: auto;
		min-height: 0;
		overflow-y: auto;
		background: var(--plate);
		padding: 12px var(--space-md) 10px;
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
	}
	.rp--app .rp-panel > * { display: flex; flex-direction: column; flex: 1 1 auto; min-height: 0; }

	/* The chart was a fixed 196px box in a 326px panel. It takes what it is given. */
	.rp--app .rp-chart-box { height: auto; flex: 1 1 auto; min-height: 150px; }

	/* The ledger scrolls, so it simply needs the height. */
	.rp--app .rp-alarms { flex: 1 1 auto; min-height: 0; overflow-y: auto; }

	/* The ledger is a LOG and scrolls by nature — 85 corrections will never fit a band. The
	   other two are readouts and must not. */
	.rp--app [data-panel='ledger'] { overflow-y: auto; }

	/* THE ANSWER TABLE FITS ITS CELL. It was the one panel scrolling inside itself, and a
	   readout you have to scroll to finish reading is not a readout — the last row is "test
	   accuracy right now", which is the row the metric strip above is echoing. Row padding
	   and type come down until five rows and a header clear the band. */
	/* Sized for the TALLEST state, not the resting one. The ORMAS cell grows to two lines
	   once corrections accumulate ("41 bounded corrections, each inside its declared
	   ceiling"), and at rest-height padding that pushed the last row — "test accuracy right
	   now" — out of the cell. */
	/* TYPE BACK UP. It was shrunk to 11px to survive the four-up row; with the record gone
	   and the panel filling, there is room to read comfortably again. The rows distribute
	   down the panel rather than bunching at the top. */
	/* 12.5px, not 13.5px. Bigger type was the point — it was 11px to survive the old
	   four-up row — but the ceiling is the TALLEST state, not the resting one: at 13.5px the
	   ORMAS cell's two lines pushed the table 76px past its panel at 1440. Measured back
	   down until six rows clear the band at the end of the run. */
	.rp--app .rp-verdict-table { font-size: 12.5px; height: 100%; }
	.rp--app .rp-verdict-table :global(tbody) { height: 100%; }
	.rp--app .rp-verdict-table :global(tbody th) { padding: 3px 10px 3px 0; font-size: 12.5px; line-height: 1.28; }
	.rp--app .rp-verdict-table :global(tbody td) { padding: 3px 10px; font-size: 12px; line-height: 1.32; }
	.rp--app .rp-chart-label { font-size: 11px; letter-spacing: 0.1em; }

	/* THE SHORTEST WINDOW PAYS, NOT EVERY WINDOW. At 900px of viewport the readout band is
	   281px and six rows of a comparison table with wrapping cells want 315px at full size.
	   Shrinking the type everywhere to satisfy the smallest screen is how it ended up at 11px
	   in the first place, so the notch is scoped to the heights that need it. */
	@media (max-height: 920px) {
		.rp--app .rp-verdict-table { font-size: 11.5px; }
		.rp--app .rp-verdict-table :global(tbody th) { padding: 2px 9px 2px 0; font-size: 11.5px; line-height: 1.25; }
		.rp--app .rp-verdict-table :global(tbody td) { padding: 2px 9px; font-size: 11px; line-height: 1.28; }
		.rp--app .rp-panel { padding: 10px var(--space-md) 8px; }
	}

	.rp--app .rp-verdict-table thead th { padding-bottom: 5px; }
	.rp--app .rp-chart-label { margin-bottom: 6px; }

	/* Two rows of two before the cells get too narrow to read, then one column. Driven by the
	   working area's own width, which is the whole viewport now that the rail is gone. */
	/* BELOW 1240 THE SCREEN STOPS BEING ONE SCREEN, and says so rather than crushing. The
	   panels wrap to two rows there, and two rows of readouts plus two network drawings do
	   not fit in the 711px a 800px window leaves — measured, each cell came out 92px tall,
	   which is not a panel. The console releases its fixed height and the page scrolls; the
	   band keeps a floor so a cell is always something you can read. */
	@media (max-width: 1400px) {
		/* The control needs the room more than the identifiers do. */
		.rp-appbar-trail, .rp-opts-label { display: none; }
	}

	@media (max-width: 1240px) {
		.rp--app { height: auto; }
		.rp-appbar { height: auto; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; padding-block: 8px; }
		.rp-appbar-run { flex-basis: 100%; text-align: left; }
		.rp--app .rp-tabs { grid-template-columns: 1.4fr 1fr; }
		.rp--app .rp-panel { min-height: 230px; }
	}
	@container (max-width: 680px) {
		.rp--app .rp-tabs { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
	}
	.rp--app .rp-anatomy > :global(svg) { height: 240px !important; }

	/* The pane stacking follows the WORKING AREA, not the viewport, because the rail's width
	   changes underneath it. .rp already declares container-type: inline-size. */
	@container (max-width: 1150px) {
		.rp--app .rp-panes { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
	}

	/* A phone is not a console. The rail becomes a scrolling strip and the screen gives up
	   its fixed height, because 560px of instrument in a 620px window is not usable. */
	@media (max-width: 860px) {
		.rp--app { height: auto; }
		.rp-metrics { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
		.rp--app .rp-panel { height: auto; min-height: 200px; }
	}

	/* ── the failure state ── */
	.rp-failed {
		margin: var(--space-md) var(--space-lg);
		padding: var(--space-md) var(--space-lg);
		border: 1px solid var(--warn);
		border-radius: 4px;
		background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--warn) 8%, transparent);
		max-width: 68ch;
	}
	.rp-failed-title { margin: 0 0 4px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--fg); }
	.rp-failed-body { margin: 0 0 var(--space-sm); font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--fg-mid); }
	/* Nothing below the bar is meaningful without the run, so it goes rather than sitting
	   there empty pretending to be an instrument. */
	.rp.is-failed .rp-plain,
	.rp.is-failed .rp-stageband,
	.rp.is-failed .rp-controls,
	.rp.is-failed .rp-tabs,
	.rp.is-failed .rp-metrics,
	.rp.is-failed .rp-recordband { display: none; }
	.rp.is-failed .rp-console,
	.rp.is-failed .rp-shell { height: auto; min-height: 0; }

	/* ── plain-language strip ── */
	.rp-plain {
		margin: 0;
		padding: 10px var(--space-lg);
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
		font-size: 13px;
		line-height: 1.5;
		color: var(--fg-mid);
		max-width: 104ch;
	}
	.rp-plain strong { color: var(--fg); font-weight: 700; }

	/* ── the orientation stripe ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
	   Deliberately NOT chrome. Everything else in this band stack is uppercase mono at
	   10-11px; this is sentence-case prose at 14-15px on a raised plate, so it reads as
	   something written rather than something labelled, and a reader can tell in one
	   glance which of the two it is.

	   The two proper nouns are tinted to the arms they name — ORMAS to the amber of the
	   open network, "sealed network" to the blue of the baseline — so the sentence binds
	   itself to the two panels below without a legend. Computed on #1A1D2E: the lead
	   14.50:1, the body 7.58:1, ORMAS 7.45:1, the sealed blue 6.49:1. */
	.rp-orient {
		background: var(--ink-raised);
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
		padding: 12px var(--space-lg);
		flex: none;
	}
	.rp-orient-line {
		margin: 0;
		max-width: 132ch;
		font-size: 14px;
		line-height: 1.5;
		color: var(--fg-mid);
	}
	.rp-orient-line strong {
		color: var(--fg);
		font-weight: 700;
		font-size: 15px;
	}
	.rp-orient-open { color: var(--ormas); font-weight: 600; }
	.rp-orient-sealed { color: var(--base); font-weight: 600; }

	/* ── tabs ── */
	.rp-tabs {
		border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
		background: var(--plate);
	}

	.rp-tablist {
		display: flex;
		gap: 2px;
		padding: 0 var(--space-lg);
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
		overflow-x: auto;
		scrollbar-width: none;
	}
	.rp-tablist::-webkit-scrollbar { display: none; }

	.rp-tab {
		font: inherit;
		font-size: var(--text-small);
		font-weight: 600;
		white-space: nowrap;
		padding: 8px 14px;
		border: 0;
		border-bottom: 2px solid transparent;
		background: none;
		color: var(--fg-dim);
		cursor: pointer;
		transition: color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out),
			border-color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out);
	}
	.rp-tab:hover { color: var(--fg); }
	.rp-tab[aria-selected='true'] {
		color: var(--ormas);
		border-bottom-color: var(--ormas);
	}

	/* NO `display` ON .rp-panel. The panels are toggled with the hidden attribute, and an
	   author display here would outrank the UA rule and make every panel permanently
	   visible — the exact bug that shipped an always-open guide card. */
	/* THE PANEL SETS THE COMPONENT'S HEIGHT, so it is fixed rather than min-height'd: every
	   tab is the same height and switching between them does not make the page jump. 244px
	   is the tallest of the four panels' contents at 1440px, measured, not chosen. */
	.rp-panel {
		padding: var(--space-md) var(--space-lg);
		height: 258px;
		overflow-y: auto;
	}

	.rp-readout {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: 1.7fr 1fr;
		gap: var(--space-lg);
		align-items: start;
	}
	.rp-chart, .rp-ledger { min-width: 0; }
	.rp-chart-label { margin: 0 0 var(--space-sm); }
	/* HEIGHT, NOT ASPECT RATIO. At 5/2 the box grew with its width: 460px tall on a 1150px
	   panel, which is nearly twice the panel it sits in. It now fills the panel it is given. */
	.rp-chart-box { position: relative; height: 196px; min-height: 0; }
	.rp-chart-box canvas { position: absolute; inset: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; }

	.rp-alarms { min-height: 150px; font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11px; }
	.rp-alarms :global(.rp-alarms-idle) { margin: 0; color: var(--fg-dim); font-style: italic; }
	.rp-alarms :global(.rp-alarms-count) {
		margin: 0 0 8px;
		font-size: var(--text-eyebrow);
		letter-spacing: 0.08em;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		color: var(--fg-dim);
	}
	.rp-alarms :global(.rp-alarm) {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: 5.2rem 4rem 1fr;
		gap: 4px 10px;
		align-items: baseline;
		margin: 0;
		padding: 4px 0;
		border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
		color: var(--fg-mid);
		font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
	}
	.rp-alarms :global(.rp-alarm.is-latest) { color: var(--fg); }
	.rp-alarms :global(.rp-alarm-step) { color: var(--fg-dim); }
	.rp-alarms :global(.rp-alarm-node) { font-weight: 700; color: var(--fg); }
	.rp-alarms :global(.rp-alarm-diag--dead) { color: var(--warn); font-weight: 700; }
	.rp-alarms :global(.rp-alarm-diag--oscillating) { color: var(--ormas); }
	.rp-alarms :global(.rp-alarm-cap) { grid-column: 2 / -1; color: var(--fg-dim); font-size: 10px; }

	/* ── the verdict ledger ───────────────────────────────────── */

	/* The site already argues this way on the home page: question, how it is
	   answered today, with ORMAS. Same three columns, answered live against the
	   frame on screen, so the difference is read rather than inferred.

	   Every rule here is scoped through .rp-verdict-table — a static element —
	   with :global on the rows, because the rows are injected by script and
	   carry no scoping attribute of their own. */
	/* ── guided read ──────────────────────────────────────────────
	   Three rules, each one written after the previous attempt failed on a real screen.

	   1. THE DOCK DOES NOT MOVE. Version one inserted the annotation beside whichever panel
	      was lit, so it jumped between three places down a component taller than the viewport
	      and the reader lost the Next button. It is now one fixed position for the whole
	      sequence and the lit panel scrolls to the reader instead.

	   2. IT LIVES ON <body>. `.rp` sets container-type: inline-size, which applies layout
	      containment and makes it the containing block for fixed descendants — a fixed card
	      inside it is fixed to the component, not the viewport. The .rp-local custom
	      properties do not travel with it, so everything here reads global tokens.

	   3. HIGHLIGHT BY ADDITION, NOT BY SUBTRACTION. Version two dropped the whole component
	      to 0.3, which reads as disabled rather than guided — the page looked broken. The
	      surroundings now recede only slightly and the lit panel is raised by an outline and
	      a lift, which is the difference between "this is off" and "look here". */
	/* The colour rules for this button live further down, beside .rp-btn--play. They were
	   here, ahead of the base .rp-btn rule, and `color: var(--fg)` won on source order at
	   equal specificity — the amber never applied and the button measured 18.23:1, which is
	   ink. Same class, same weight, later wins. */

	/* Leaf panels only: .rp-ledger is a child of .rp-readout and opacity composites down the
	   tree, so dimming the parent would cap the lit child at whatever the parent is. */
	.rp.is-guided .rp-panes,
	.rp.is-guided .rp-chart,
	.rp.is-guided .rp-ledger,
	.rp.is-guided .rp-verdict,
	.rp.is-guided .rp-bar {
		opacity: 0.55;
		transition: opacity 220ms ease;
	}
	.rp.is-guided .is-lit {
		opacity: 1;
		position: relative;
		z-index: 1;
		border-radius: 4px;
		outline: 2px solid color-mix(in oklab, var(--ormas) 70%, transparent);
		outline-offset: 10px;
		background: var(--plate);
		box-shadow: 0 0 0 10px var(--plate), 0 10px 32px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.08);
	}

	/* ── the dock ── */
	.rp-guide:not([hidden]) {
		position: fixed;
		z-index: 90;                       /* under the nav (100) and its menus (120) */
		left: 50%;
		transform: translateX(-50%);
		bottom: 18px;
		width: min(1240px, calc(100vw - 32px));
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
		grid-template-rows: auto auto;
		align-items: center;
		column-gap: var(--space-lg);
		row-gap: 12px;
		padding: 14px var(--space-lg) 16px;
		border: 1px solid color-mix(in oklab, var(--chart-ormas) 30%, var(--border-hairline));
		border-radius: 10px;
		background: var(--bg-surface);
		box-shadow: 0 12px 40px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.16);
		color: var(--text-primary);
		/* Measured tallest is 224px on a 390×844 phone and 158px on a 1280×640 laptop, so this
		   never engages in practice. It is here for the viewport nobody tested: a long stop on
		   a short window must scroll inside the dock rather than push Next off the bottom. */
		max-height: 46vh;
		overflow-y: auto;
	}

	/* THE RAIL. Full width across the top, one segment per stop, clickable. */
	.rp-guide-rail {
		grid-column: 1 / -1;
		grid-row: 1;
		display: grid;
		grid-auto-flow: column;
		grid-auto-columns: 1fr;
		gap: 4px;
	}
	.rp-guide :global(.rp-guide-seg) {
		appearance: none;
		border: 0;
		padding: 0;
		height: 3px;
		border-radius: 2px;
		background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--text-muted) 28%, transparent);
		cursor: pointer;
		transition: background 180ms ease, height 180ms ease;
	}
	.rp-guide :global(.rp-guide-seg[data-done='true']) {
		background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--chart-ormas) 45%, transparent);
	}
	.rp-guide :global(.rp-guide-seg[aria-current='true']) {
		background: var(--chart-ormas);
		height: 5px;
	}
	.rp-guide :global(.rp-guide-seg:hover) { background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--chart-ormas) 70%, transparent); }

	.rp-guide-text { grid-column: 1; grid-row: 2; min-width: 0; }
	.rp-guide-eyebrow {
		font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
		font-size: 11px;
		letter-spacing: 0.08em;
		color: var(--chart-ormas);
		margin: 0 0 3px;
	}
	.rp-guide-title { margin: 0 0 4px; font-size: var(--text-body); line-height: 1.25; }
	.rp-guide-body {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: var(--text-small);
		line-height: 1.5;
		color: var(--text-secondary);
		max-width: 92ch;
	}

	/* Fixed column, so Close / Back / Next are in the same pixel at every stop — the thing
	   the reader was hunting for when the card used to move. */
	.rp-guide-foot {
		grid-column: 2;
		grid-row: 2;
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 10px;
	}
	/* SKIP IS A CONTROL, not a word. It was a bare text link the colour of a caption, on a
	   panel that opens itself — the one thing a reader who does not want a tour needs to
	   find first. It is a bordered button now, with the key that also does it printed on it. */
	.rp-guide-skip {
		display: inline-flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 7px;
		font: inherit;
		font-size: var(--text-small);
		background: none;
		border: 1px solid var(--border-hairline);
		border-radius: 4px;
		padding: 8px 12px;
		color: var(--text-secondary);
		cursor: pointer;
		transition: border-color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out), color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out);
	}
	.rp-guide-skip:hover { color: var(--text-primary); border-color: var(--border-strong); }
	.rp-guide-esc {
		font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
		font-size: 9.5px;
		letter-spacing: 0.06em;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		color: var(--text-muted);
		border: 1px solid var(--border-hairline);
		border-radius: 3px;
		padding: 1px 4px;
	}
	.rp-guide :global(.rp-btn) {
		font: inherit;
		font-size: var(--text-small);
		padding: 9px 20px;
		border: 1px solid var(--border-hairline);
		border-radius: 4px;
		background: var(--bg-surface);
		color: var(--text-primary);
		cursor: pointer;
		white-space: nowrap;
	}
	.rp-guide :global(.rp-btn:disabled) { opacity: 0.35; cursor: default; }
	/* #fff, not --text-on-dark. The cream is #F3F1EA and on this amber it measures 4.34:1,
	   just under the floor; white is 4.91:1 on the same fill. The dock is light-themed and
	   sits outside the console, so it does not inherit the console's amber. */
	.rp-guide :global(.rp-btn--primary) {
		background: var(--chart-ormas);
		border-color: var(--chart-ormas);
		color: #fff;
	}

	/* TRAVELLING. The run takes several seconds to cross from one stop to the next, and the
	   first version hid the dock for the whole of it — a dimmed component with no controls,
	   which is indistinguishable from a crash. The dock now stays, says what it is doing, and
	   the live segment fills with the epochs so the wait reads as motion. */
	.rp-guide-live {
		display: none;
		margin-left: 10px;
		padding-left: 10px;
		border-left: 1px solid var(--border-hairline);
		color: var(--text-muted);
		font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
	}
	.rp-guide.is-travelling .rp-guide-live { display: inline; }
	.rp-guide.is-travelling .rp-guide-title,
	.rp-guide.is-travelling .rp-guide-body { opacity: 0.45; transition: opacity 200ms ease; }
	.rp-guide.is-travelling :global(.rp-guide-seg[aria-current='true']) {
		background: linear-gradient(
			to right,
			var(--chart-ormas) var(--travel, 0%),
			color-mix(in oklab, var(--chart-ormas) 20%, transparent) var(--travel, 0%)
		);
	}

	/* ONLY WHERE THE GUTTER ACTUALLY FITS IT. A 360px panel needs 24px of clearance from the
	   1280px container and 32px from the viewport edge, so the gutter has to be ≥416px — a
	   viewport of 2112px. At 1920px the gutter is 320px and a side panel would sit on top of
	   the thing it is describing, which is the bug this replaced. */
	@media (min-width: 2120px) {
		.rp-guide:not([hidden]) {
			left: auto;
			right: calc((100vw - 1280px) / 2 - 384px);
			transform: none;
			top: 50%;
			translate: 0 -50%;
			bottom: auto;
			width: 360px;
			grid-template-columns: 1fr;
			row-gap: 14px;
		}
		.rp-guide-text, .rp-guide-foot { grid-column: 1; }
		.rp-guide-foot { grid-row: 3; justify-content: space-between; }
	}

	@media (max-width: 760px) {
		.rp-guide:not([hidden]) {
			grid-template-columns: 1fr;
			bottom: 8px;
			width: calc(100vw - 16px);
			padding: 12px 14px 14px;
		}
		.rp-guide-text, .rp-guide-foot { grid-column: 1; }
		.rp-guide-foot { grid-row: 3; justify-content: space-between; }
		.rp-guide-body {
			font-size: 13px;
			display: -webkit-box;
			-webkit-line-clamp: 4;
			line-clamp: 4;
			-webkit-box-orient: vertical;
			overflow: hidden;
		}
	}

	@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
		.rp.is-guided .rp-panes,
		.rp.is-guided .rp-chart,
		.rp.is-guided .rp-ledger,
		.rp.is-guided .rp-verdict,
		.rp.is-guided .rp-bar { transition: none; }
		.rp-guide :global(.rp-guide-seg) { transition: none; }
	}

	.rp-verdict { padding: 0; }

	.rp-verdict-table {
		width: 100%;
		border-collapse: collapse;
		table-layout: fixed;
		margin-top: var(--space-sm);
	}

	.rp-verdict-table thead th {
		text-align: left;
		padding: 0 12px 7px;
		font-size: var(--text-eyebrow);
		letter-spacing: 0.09em;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		color: var(--fg-dim);
		font-weight: 600;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-strong);
	}
	.rp-verdict-table thead th:first-child { padding-left: 0; width: 30%; }
	.rp-verdict-table thead th:nth-child(2) { width: 30%; }
	.rp-verdict-table thead th:nth-child(3) { color: var(--ormas); width: 40%; }

	.rp-verdict-table :global(tbody th) {
		text-align: left;
		padding: 5px 12px 5px 0;
		font-weight: 500;
		font-size: var(--text-small);
		color: var(--fg);
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
		vertical-align: baseline;
	}
	.rp-verdict-table :global(tbody td) {
		text-align: left;
		padding: 5px 12px;
		font-family: var(--mono);
		font-size: 11.5px;
		line-height: 1.45;
		color: var(--fg-mid);
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
		vertical-align: baseline;
		font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
	}

	/* The ORMAS column carries a faint ground, so the answer side is one
	   continuous object rather than three unrelated cells per row. */
	.rp-verdict-table :global(tbody td:last-child) {
		background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ormas) 5%, transparent);
		color: var(--fg);
	}
	.rp-verdict-table thead th:nth-child(3) {
		background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ormas) 5%, transparent);
	}

	/* An unanswerable cell is a statement about the architecture, not a missing
	   value, so it is set in muted italic and led by a rule rather than a dash —
	   a dash reads as "not measured yet". */
	.rp-verdict :global(.rp-v-none) {
		color: var(--fg-dim);
		font-style: italic;
	}
	.rp-verdict :global(.rp-v-none)::before {
		content: '';
		display: inline-block;
		width: 10px;
		height: 1px;
		margin-right: 7px;
		vertical-align: middle;
		background: currentColor;
	}
	.rp-verdict :global(.rp-v-hit) { color: var(--ormas); font-weight: 700; }
	/* The one row both can answer, marked identically on both sides. */
	.rp-verdict :global(.rp-v-both) { color: var(--fg); font-weight: 700; font-size: 13px; }

	/* ── the beat ─────────────────────────────────────────────── */

	.rp-beat {
		margin: 0;
		padding: var(--space-sm) var(--space-lg);
		border-top: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--warn) 45%, transparent);
		border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--warn) 45%, transparent);
		background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--warn) 6%, transparent);
		font-size: var(--text-small);
		color: var(--fg);
	}

	/* ── controls ─────────────────────────────────────────────── */

	.rp-controls {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		gap: var(--space-sm) var(--space-md);
		align-items: center;
		padding: var(--space-md) var(--space-lg);
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
		background: var(--plate-sunk);
	}

	.rp-btn {
		font: inherit;
		font-size: var(--text-small);
		padding: 6px 12px;
		border: 1px solid var(--rule);
		border-radius: 3px;
		background: var(--plate);
		color: var(--fg);
		cursor: pointer;
	}
	.rp-btn:hover { border-color: var(--border-strong); }
	/* ── THE THREE ACTIONS ────────────────────────────────────────
	   A first-time visitor arrives at a still frame and has no reason to think any of this
	   moves. All three controls were the same cream chrome as the speed toggles, so nothing
	   said "start here". They are not equal, though, and treating them equally would have
	   produced three shouts and no hierarchy:

	     Play                  the one thing to do first   — solid amber
	     Guided read           the way in, if Play is not  — amber outline
	     Replay from the event for someone already reading — ink outline, quiet

	   Amber, not indigo: inside this component amber is the ORMAS arm, the colour its line is
	   drawn in on every chart. Measured on the white plate — #A5600C carries white text at
	   4.91:1 and separates from the plate at 4.91:1, so it works as a fill and as an outline. */
	.rp-btn--play {
		min-width: 6.5rem;
		font-weight: 700;
		background: var(--ormas);
		border-color: var(--ormas);
		/* INK, NOT WHITE. The light-theme amber was dark enough to carry white at 4.91:1; the
		   console amber is a light one and white on it measured 2.24:1. The label takes the
		   panel's own ground instead, which is 8.01:1 and correct on both themes because the
		   fill and the text move together. */
		color: var(--plate);
		display: inline-flex;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: center;
		gap: 7px;
	}
	.rp-btn--play:hover { filter: brightness(1.08); }

	/* A glyph, so the control is recognisable before the label is read. Driven by the class
	   the island already toggles on the root, so there is no second source of truth about
	   whether the run is playing. */
	.rp-btn--play::before {
		content: '\25B6';
		font-size: 9px;
		line-height: 1;
	}
	.rp.is-running .rp-btn--play::before { content: '\275A\275A'; font-size: 8px; }

	/* THE ATTENTION RING, and the two things that stop it being an irritation: it exists only
	   while the run is NOT playing, so the first press ends it permanently for that visit, and
	   it is a ring on a pseudo-element rather than a transform, so it cannot shift the layout
	   of a row measured this tightly. */
	@keyframes rp-play-attention {
		0%, 68%  { box-shadow: 0 0 0 0 color-mix(in oklab, var(--ormas) 55%, transparent); }
		84%      { box-shadow: 0 0 0 7px color-mix(in oklab, var(--ormas) 0%, transparent); }
		100%     { box-shadow: 0 0 0 0 color-mix(in oklab, var(--ormas) 0%, transparent); }
	}
	.rp:not(.is-running) .rp-btn--play {
		animation: rp-play-attention 2600ms var(--ease-out) infinite;
	}

	.rp-btn--guide {
		border-color: var(--ormas);
		color: var(--ormas-text);
		font-weight: 600;
	}
	.rp-btn--guide:hover { background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--ormas) 9%, transparent); }
	/* 8%, not 14%. The active tint is behind amber text, and at 14% it took the label to
	   3.73:1. The state still reads — it is the only tinted control in the row. */
	.rp.is-guided .rp-btn--guide {
		background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--ormas) 8%, transparent);
	}

	.rp-btn--event {
		border-color: var(--fg-dim);
		color: var(--fg);
		font-weight: 600;
	}
	.rp-btn--event:hover { border-color: var(--fg); }

	@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
		.rp:not(.is-running) .rp-btn--play { animation: none; }
	}
	.rp-btn--primary { background: var(--accent-dark); border-color: var(--accent-dark); color: #fff; font-weight: 600; }

	.rp-scrub { flex: 1 1 200px; display: block; }
	.rp-scrub input { width: 100%; accent-color: var(--ormas); }
	.rp-epoch { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11px; color: var(--fg-dim); min-width: 10ch; }
	/* Says what kind of run this is when there is no event to wait for. */
	.rp-nature { margin: 0; flex-basis: 100%; font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--fg-dim); }
	.rp-nature:empty { display: none; }

	.rp-rates { display: flex; gap: 2px; }
	.rp-rate {
		font: inherit; font-size: 11px; font-family: var(--mono);
		padding: 5px 8px;
		border: 1px solid var(--rule);
		background: var(--plate); color: var(--fg-dim); cursor: pointer;
	}
	.rp-rate[aria-pressed='true'] { color: var(--plate); background: var(--fg); font-weight: 700; }

	/* ── the record ───────────────────────────────────────────── */

	.rp-record {
		padding: 0;
		background: var(--plate-sunk);
	}
	.rp-record-head { margin-bottom: var(--space-md); }
	/* ── THE RECORD STRIP ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
	   Three named groups across the top — the run, the event, the recovery — then the table.
	   As a flat auto-fit grid of nine cells this wrapped raggedly and gave every figure the
	   same weight, which is how a record of "a network broke and repaired itself" ended up
